AB  - Sea lice (Lepeophtheirus salmonis), or salmon lice, naturally affect farmed salmonids, posing a significant global concern for Atlantic salmon, farmed and wild. Understanding how sea lice infestation patterns vary temporally and spatially among aquaculture sites can be a key for effective management of the parasite. Various analytical approaches have been used to better understand the dynamics of sea lice populations on aquaculture sites. The objective of this study was to explore the utility of applying the dynamic factor analysis (DFA) to time series data from an isolated subset of sites of the Eastern Canadian aquaculture industry to estimate common patterns of sea lice infestations among farmed salmon sites. The results showed that the DFA models, with three common trends, varied bath treatment effects among sites, and unequal non-diagonal variance-covariance matrix for observation errors had a reasonable fit to the data. The analysis highlighted the relative importance of inter- and intra-site sources of infestations and identified sites that share sea lice populations and those that do not. This research concludes that estimating common trends for sea lice infestations on aquaculture sites provides supplemental information about the connectivity of sea lice populations on aquaculture sites that is not explained by other known factors.
