AB  - Organophosphate esters are flame retardants applied to commercial products, but they have been detected in food due to contamination from the environment or food packaging. Currently, risk assessment only considers raw food samples, but most food humans consumed are cooked. The process also only considers the parent OPE compounds. Therefore, the objectives of this study were to investigate the thermal degradation of six representative OPEs (TBOEP, TPHP, TDCIPP and their respective diesters) in water, honey, chicken breast and salmon, and to elucidate their degradation products during the cooking process. In a model aqueous solution, it was found that triester OPEs degraded significantly at 100 °C and formed their diesters by hydrolysis, but degradation was not observed at 70 °C. TPHP had the highest degradation rate, with a half-life of 29.9 minutes. The rate of degradation was slower in the three food matrices, and the trend was different than what was observed in the model solution. Non-targeted analysis was applied to identify unknown degradation products found within the different heated matrices, where a range of unknown compounds was formed during the heating process. This study showed that the degradation reactions were specific to each OPE and the food matrix involved.
