AB  - ... ... The AFS Idaho Chapter (ICAFS) held its 61st Annual Meeting jointly with the AFS Washington‐British Columbia (WA‐BC) Chapter. The meeting was held at the Centennial Hotel in Spokane, Washington from April 29 to May 2, 2024, and was a tremendous success with a whopping 420 registrants, including nearly 70 students from 13 universities, and 10 retirees. This was a rare and unique opportunity to work across state, international, and Chapter lines and certainly made the meeting more interesting, inspirational, and educational. The meeting kicked off with a full day of educational opportunities. Workshop topics included genetics, lamprey conservation, nutrition in aquaculture, predicting salmon runs, PIT tagging, communicating science, and aquatic connectivity. Attendance at the seven workshops exceeded 180 people. A tremendous thanks to our dedicated instructors who invested boat loads of time and energy in preparing these highly educational workshops. Lauren Andrews, ICAFS President‐Elect and WA‐BC President‐Elect Sean Simmons organized a diverse group of plenary speakers to address the meeting theme of “Returning Home: Anadromous Fisheries in the Pacific Northwest.” Presentations from plenary speakers Monica Tonasket, Jay Hesse and Russ Thurow, who copresented, Jonathan Armstrong, Christopher Caudill, and Scott Hinch discussed the importance and cultural significance of Pacific salmon Oncorhynchus spp. to Spokane Tribal members, the urgency of rebuilding runs in the Snake River basin, as well as observations and concerns related to the adaptive abilities of salmonids to respond to climate change. As was intended, the plenary session set the stage for the remainder of the meeting by inspiring the audience to take on these daunting challenges using creative fisheries science as well as public and policy maker education and engagement. The remainder of the meeting included 19 symposia, comprising 199 contributed talks, 32 posters, business meetings, and a spawning run.
