AB  - <jats:p>Sustainable aquaculture management depends on evidence-based fallowing regimes, yet optimal recovery periods for high-latitude marine environments remain inadequately defined. We present a longitudinal analysis of benthic ecosystem dynamics (2002–2022) in Icelandic salmon farming areas, combining long-term monitoring with an intensive 18-month reciprocal field experiment. Two contrasting sites underwent opposite production transitions: Glimeyri (peak production to fallowing) and Hamraborg (fallowing to peak production), creating a paired experiment controlling for seasonal and environmental variability. Sampling comprised macrofaunal community analysis (n = 280), biogeochemical measurements (redox potential, pH, sulphides) and organic enrichment parameters (C, N, P) across seven events. Results demonstrated predictable recovery trajectories across multiple production cycles (p &lt; 0.001). Multivariate community analysis revealed three distinct succession phases during recovery: (1) opportunistic dominance (0–4 months, Shannon H’ = 1.2–1.8); (2) transitional community (4–12 months, H’ = 1.8–2.5); and (3) climax community approximating reference conditions (12–18 months, H’ = 2.5–3.5). A highly significant logarithmic relationship was documented between recovery time and Shannon diversity (r² = 0.87, p &lt; 0.0001), with recovery rates averaging 0.12–0.15 H’ units/month during the initial phase but declining to 0.05–0.08 units/month in later stages. Companion papers address horizontal spatial distribution and vertical sediment profiles. These findings provide the first robust recovery models empirically derived for high-latitude aquaculture management.</jats:p>
