AB  - <jats:p>The rational selection of a water supply source is one of the key factors determining the efficiency of industrial rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss Walbaum, 1792) farming. Water temperature and hydrochemical characteristics directly influence fish physiological condition, growth rate, survival, and overall productivity of aquaculture systems. Therefore, the development of technologically and economically justified water supply schemes ensuring optimal rearing conditions is of particular importance.The object of the study was rainbow trout cultivated in a pond-based aquaculture system of the agricultural cooperative “TroutKargaly” located in the Aktobe region of the Republic of Kazakhstan. The aim of the study was to comparatively assess the efficiency of trout farming under different water supply sources. Two technological schemes were analyzed: the use of artesian water and the application of mixed water formed by combining artesian water with water from the Kargaly reservoir.During the experiment, fish growth dynamics, average daily weight gain, survival rate, as well as temperature regime and key hydrochemical parameters were monitored. The results demonstrated that artesian water is characterized by stable hydrochemical properties, providing favorable environmental conditions. However, its relatively low temperature limits the intensity of fish growth.It was found that the use of a mixed water supply system contributes to the formation of a more optimal temperature regime while maintaining acceptable hydrochemical parameters. This positively affects growth performance and overall production efficiency.The findings indicate that combined water supply is a technologically justified approach in pond-based trout farming, allowing for increased biological productivity and improved economic performance of aquaculture enterprises.</jats:p>
