AB  - <jats:p>A comparative analysis was conducted on the proximate nutritional composition of wild and farmed fish species, specifically Common Carp (Cyprinus carpio) and Rainbow Trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss), collected from the Swabi District of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan. Fifty samples (25 wild and 25 farmed) were analyzed for protein, lipid, moisture, ash, and carbohydrate content using standard AOAC (2005) procedures. Statistically significant differences (p &lt; 0.05) were observed across all nutritional parameters between wild and farmed. Wild fish demonstrated higher levels of protein (18.55 ± 1.03%), moisture (72.22 ± 1.28%), and ash (1.20 ± 0.19%), while farmed fish contained significantly greater concentrations of lipid (7.84 ± 1.26%) and carbohydrate (2.88 ± 0.44%). Independent t-tests confirmed that these differences were highly significant (p &lt; 0.001 for most parameters). In contrast, one-way ANOVA indicated no significant nutritional variation between species (p &gt; 0.05), suggesting that the rearing environment exerted a greater influence on fish composition than species differ. These findings underscore the nutritional advantages of wild fish and indicate the necessity for improved feed strategies and environmental management in aquaculture systems to enhance the quality of farmed fish. This research provides valuable data for Pakistan’s aquaculture sector and offers evidence-based recommendations to optimize fish farming practices in accordance with nutritional standards and public health requirements.</jats:p>
