AB  - This chapter explores relevant features related to the parasite Caligus rogercresseyi, currently the most important pathogen affecting the salmon industry of Chile, being a permanent threat. Its geographic distribution ranges from southern Chile to Argentina, being present in salmonid and native fishes of both countries. In Chile, it is also common on Eleginops maclovinus, which is a native fish that interacts closely with the salmon in farms, perpetuating infestations. Physical damage on fish produced by the parasite is characterized by skin injuries, loss of the integrity of the skin, peeling and petechial haemorrhage, among other injuries. This chapter updates the knowledge about this parasitosis, addressing the life cycle, transmission and distribution, parasite diagnosis, pharmacological and non-pharmacological tools and economic impact.
