AB  - In recent years, debates over genetically engineered salmon, net-pen fish farming, and hatcheries in the Pacific Northwest have often foregrounded the need to protect the genetic “purity” of wild salmon and steelhead. We use Shotwell’s notion of purity politics to suggest that this focus on the purity of wild fish genetics is rooted in colonial conceptions of nature and may ultimately undercut important legal protections for anadromous fish runs. Efforts to maintain and propagate “wild” DNA are beneficial, inasmuch as these efforts are about maintaining the uniquely resilient and adaptive capacities of salmon and steelhead. By attending to the purity politics embedded within much of the resistance to GE salmon, hatchery fish, and net pen fish farming, we demonstrate the need for a more complex and nuanced argument around the protection of wild runs and for guidance by Indigenous frameworks that understand fisheries management as a matter of relationality.
