AB  - Gyrodactylus salaris and Gyrodactylus derjavinoides are monogenean parasites on fins and skin of certain salmonid species. They are hermaphroditic, viviparous and readily spread in vulnerable host populations. G. salaris was introduced to Norway in the 1970s and subsequently spread to 53 rivers with Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar). Most Baltic salmon strains have high natural resistance, but East Atlantic salmon strains are very susceptible to infection, which leads to severe stock depletion. Continuous monitoring of parasite occurrence is recommended. Control strategies are needed to eradicate these parasites, but some chemical methods have had ecological impacts. G. derjavinoides targets brown trout (Salmo trutta) in natural streams and rivers and rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) in farms, which may call for control measures. However, the two host species are also able to mount protective mucosal responses on fins and skin to prevent epidemic outbreaks.
