AB  - Aquaculture plays an increasingly crucial role in supplying fish for human consumption. Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) is one of the most recognized and economically important species in the Salmonidae family. Understanding the genetic diversity of Atlantic salmon requires a representative population that captures global variation. Here, we assembled a worldwide panel of 1,399 individuals and identified over 22.3 million genome-wide SNPs through whole-genome resequencing. Genetic differentiation between S. salar genetic resources was assessed using the fixation index (Fst). The 12k loci with the highest genetic differentiation index from each genetic resource were selected, and a gradient-boosting tree model was employed to assess the importance of these loci in the genetic identification of germplasm. In addition, we compared the genotype imputation accuracy between autoencoder and Hidden Markov model (HMM)-based methods (Beagle5 and Glimpse2). The results indicate that each population possesses loci that exhibit near-complete differentiation. Convolutional neural networks proved the most effective machine learning model for identifying S. salar genetic resources, achieving 94.75% accuracy with 500 SNPs. The autoencoder-based method exhibited an average imputation accuracy of 83.77 ± 7.59% for the ground truth genotypes, compared to average imputation accuracy of 81.78 ± 8.45% and 84.01 ± 8.51% for the HMM-based imputation methods. Finally, we developed a user-friendly web-based platform (http://salmonbreeding.bbt8.cn:11003/) that enables efficient identification of S. salar genetic resources and accurate genotype imputation, with the capacity for continuous expansion of its underlying database. By comprehensively cataloging genetic variants across globally diverse populations, our work establishes the essential first step toward functional annotation of the Atlantic salmon genome. This population-scale variant atlas provides a foundational resource for future efforts to FarmGTEx, facilitating mechanistic interpretation of regulatory variation and genotype–phenotype relationships in farmed fish. Together, the platform and variant resources offer a fast, accurate, and cost-effective solution that supports conservation, breeding innovation, and the sustainable development of the Atlantic salmon industry.
