AB  - High mortality rates in Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) aquaculture remain a critical issue in Norway, with 62.8 million deaths registered during the sea phase in 2024. Improving fish health is therefore a priority for the industry, both for ethical and productivity reasons. Current health assessments rely on invasive sampling of a small number of individuals, which may not accurately reflect the overall population in sea cages. Automated sensor technologies offer a promising, non-invasive alternative, but their representativity may depend on sensor placement if fish are, as we hypothesized, vertically stratified by health status or size. In this case study, stereovision rigs and an automated camera sensor were deployed at four depths (1-14 m) in a commercial sea cage over a two-month period following a delousing operation. Fish were measured and assessed for six health indicators following the Laksvel protocol. While the overall physical health of the fish was good, findings suggest a long-term vertical stratification of salmon with chronic deviations and a temporary stratification of salmon displaying injury-based deviations. These results highlight the importance of sensor depth for representative health and size monitoring and call for further studies across seasons and under varying environmental conditions.
