AB  - As climate change and environmental pressures increase, salmon farming is expanding from sheltered coastal waters into more exposed offshore locations. These areas experience stronger and constantly changing water currents, which may affect how farmed fish swim, use energy, and cope with their environment. Understanding how salmon respond to these changing conditions is important for designing offshore cages, improving feeding strategies, and ensuring good fish welfare. My research investigated how Atlantic and Chinook salmon handle unsteady flow conditions (where water speed changes over time) that resemble those found offshore. This was compared with how they handle steady flow conditions where water speed does not change with time. I used a combination of swim-tunnel experiments and tank trials, some with fish carrying surgically implanted sensors that measured their heart rate and movement. These tools allowed me to study how fish adjust their swimming, how hard their hearts work, and how much energy they use under different flow conditions.I found that salmon use more energy when swimming under unsteady flow compared to steady flow conditions at the same speeds. Despite this, their heart rate did not always rise accordingly. In many cases, heart rate remained high but relatively stable, even when the fish were working harder. This suggests that heart rate alone is not a reliable indicator of energy use for salmon, especially in unsteady flow conditions. Movement-based measurements, such as acceleration, proved to be stronger and more consistent indicators of how much effort the fish were exerting. I also examined the long-term effects of exercise in different flow conditions. Salmon trained under unsteady flow conditions grew just as well as those trained under steady flow conditions, and both groups outperformed fish kept in low-flow conditions. This shows that salmon can adapt to changing water movement without compromising growth. In free-swimming Chinook salmon, heart rate and movement followed daily patterns and were higher under unsteady flow conditions compared to steady flow conditions. These findings suggest that offshore environments may influence activity and energy use throughout the day and night. Overall, my work highlights that movement sensors offer a more accurate way to monitor salmon energy use than heart rate alone. These insights can help guide offshore aquaculture towards better site selection, cage design, and monitoring technologies, ultimately supporting more sustainable and welfare-focused fish farming as the industry moves into the open ocean.
