AB  - <jats:p>Understanding how different aquafeed ingredients influence nutrient pathways is essential to improve feed efficiency and reduce environmental impacts. Although aquafeeds are typically formulated to meet the nutritional demands of fish and ensure cost-effectiveness, nutrient absorption and waste production are influenced by complex metabolic processes. To examine these nutrient dynamics, we conducted a 13-week growth experiment on Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar). We integrated stoichiometric and stable isotope approach to track nutrient transformation from feed to fish to faeces across four treatments that maximised isotopic contrast: a 100% fishmeal-based basal mix (control), and three test feeds containing 30% soy protein concentrate, chicken poultry concentrate, or single cell protein blended with 70% of the basal mix. All treatments supported similar growth, indicating nutrient adequacy. C:N ratio of soy protein concentrate feed was higher than the other test feeds, while fish maintained consistent C:N ratios across treatments, indicating stoichiometric homeostasis. Faecal C:N ratio varied, with chicken poultry concentrate producing the highest nitrogen waste. Distinct δ13C and δ15N signatures across feed, fish, and faeces revealed differences in nutrient assimilation. Additionally, feed–faeces isotope spacing revealed differences in gut processing of feeds. Faecal isotopes from chicken poultry concentrate closely matched the feed, whereas the other three feeds showed varying levels of divergence. By linking growth performance with stoichiometric and isotopic patterns, our findings demonstrate how these tools can identify ingredient-specific nutrient transformations and trace waste origins, supporting the development of environmentally sustainable aquafeeds.</jats:p>
