AB  - In the marine space of southern South America, nomadic people of the sea have experienced during the last century the imposition of material and immaterial boundaries that have limited their mobility. Although in the past these boundaries led to their immobilisation, currently Indigenous marine groups are using spatial boundaries to regain control over their marine space, resources, and mobility. In this article, I use the concepts of immobilisation, remobilisation, and demobilisation to examine the changing relation between spatial boundaries and mobility at sea. I show how Kawésqar people in the Chilean Patagonia are currently using spatial boundaries of the state to challenge further marine territorialisation by the state and marine salmon farming industry.
