AB  - This paper covers the rapid development of aquaculture in Chile during the last 30 years and shows how Integrated Multi-Trophic Aquaculture (IMTA) concepts were developed. The fish farming industry, predominately based on the cultivation of salmon species, has faced the substantial consequences of virus and bacterial diseases, and sea lice infections, requiring high levels of antibiotics and different chemical compounds to control these pests. High mortality losses due to different pathogens and harmful algal blooms have raised criticism from environmentally oriented organisations. Dealing with the environmental consequences of fed aquaculture is complex; IMTA offers solutions for recycling organic suspended matter and inorganic dissolved nutrients produced by fish metabolism. In this context, during the late 80s, the first approach to developing more environmentally friendly aquaculture models. A first approach using a land-based salmon-oyster-seaweed model was developed. Other studies have been conducted in the open sea, developing a model considering Atlantic salmon, molluscs, and seaweeds. These studies established the recycling efficiency that organisms can have and that it is technically feasible to introduce IMTA approaches. However, economic and regulation constraints still exist. Therefore, a more environmentally sustainable aquaculture scenario must still wait for modifications to the societal context to be properly installed.
