AB  - It is unfortunate for historical research to unexpectedly become topical. This work can be an announcement of the end of violence, for example, but often it will have reiterated a call that seems to have gone unanswered, to bear witness to a familiar violence once more. In the report of the First Congress of the Cities of Asylum in 1996, Christian Salmon mentions that the stabbing of Nobel Laureate Naguib Mahfouz on 14 October 1994 – an attack that would paralyze his right arm – was one event motivating the foundation of a network of asylum cities by the International Parliament of Writers (IPW). Accused of “blasphemy” by his assailant, the motive for the attack on Mahfouz was likely the 1989 death sentence issued by Sheikh Omar Abdel-Rahman, mimicking the fatwa of Ayatollah Khomeini against Salman Rushdie upon publication of The Satanic Verses (Najjar 139–41, 160). Abdel-Rahman declared that Mahfouz ought to be punished for his novel Awlad Haratina (Children of the Alley), published thirty years prior (Rateb 115). Today, this history is not necessarily repeating itself. Yet, it is subject to a nightmarish iteration. On 12 August 2022, Salman Rushdie was stabbed on stage moments before he could give a talk for the Chautauqua Institution for their series entitled, “More than Shelter: Redefining the American Home.” This talk was already a reminder of the violence facing writers and public intellectuals alongside people globally who are targeted, and who fear violence today. They are not unlike the conditions that the IPW responded to thirty years prior. Their charter – passed in 1994 to establish a network of cities of asylum for writers – was penned by Rushdie, who was its president from 1994 to 1997.
