AB  - Atlantic salmon eggs are typically fertilized within an hour of being extracted from the female, a process known as stripping. Extending the time between stripping and fertilization offers several benefits, including the ability to receive disease screening results before deciding which egg batches to fertilize. Storing unfertilized eggs is one method to extend this time, which is particularly appealing to commercial producers due to the increased flexibility it could generate. To study how long unfertilized Atlantic salmon eggs can be stored in vitro , two fluids were tested (ovarian fluid and artificial fluid (modified Cortland)) for up to 12 days in a commercial refrigerator (6.63 °C ± 0.62). The eggs of 10 females submitted to these conditions were then individually followed until just before first feeding, approximately 920 degree-days. The results suggest that Atlantic salmon eggs can be stored in ovarian fluid for up to four days without significant quality loss. After this period, their quality declined from a median hatching rate of over 84 % to 41.4 % by day eight of storage, and variability increased. When necessary, artificial fluid can be used to store eggs for a day maintaining a median hatching rate of around 80 %. The combination of ovarian and artificial fluid was not tested in this study, but it could be interesting to test the addition of artificial fluid when there is not enough ovarian fluid to cover all the eggs during storage. • In vitro storage of Atlantic salmon eggs was tested for up to 12 days in ovarian and artificial fluid (Cortland). • Eggs stored in ovarian fluid maintained viability for up to four days without quality loss. • After four days, hatching rates declined from over 84 % to 41 % by day eight. • Artificial fluid preserved egg viability for one day, maintaining around 80 % hatching rate.
