AB  - Marine habitats will face a growing number of pressures in the coming decades because of anthropogenically induced climate change, ocean warming and ocean acidification, biodiversity shifts and overexploitation (Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change 2023). Effective engagement of scientific research beyond academia is crucial for maximising the resilience of nature to these global challenges (Herbert-Read et al. 2022). Ocean literacy is a fundamental first step towards global marine science education, research and effective conservation of ecologically important ecosystems (Santoro et al. 2017; National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration 2013). Here, we provide some examples (not an exhaustive list) of recent outreach initiatives across multiple media types that were designed to elevate public knowledge about coralline algae—globally distributed calcified red seaweeds—and their associated habitats, known as coralline algae reefs, maerl beds or rhodolith beds. Placed within the Intergovernmental Oceanographic Commission of UNESCO (IOC/UNESCO) Ocean Literacy Framework (Santoro et al. 2017) and aligned to the seven ‘Essential Principles of Ocean Literacy’ (Box 1), we show that coralline algae are an excellent basis for elevating ocean literacy at local, regional and global scales and transcending diverse public groups. An ocean-literate person understands the Essential Principles and Fundamental Concepts about the ocean; can communicate about the ocean in a meaningful way; and is able to make informed and responsible decisions regarding the ocean and its resources. Principle 1: Earth has one big ocean with many features. Principle 2: The ocean and life in the ocean shape the features of Earth. Principle 3: The ocean is a major influence on weather and climate. Principle 4: The ocean makes Earth habitable. Principle 5: The ocean supports a great diversity of life and ecosystems. Principle 6: The ocean and humans are inextricably interconnected. Principle 7: The ocean is largely unexplored. Coralline algae are present in all the ocean basins with global distribution. Public engagement centred around coralline algae therefore opens up the opportunity for bringing in global scale ocean literacy concepts. In 2022, Costa et al. published an accessible introduction to rhodoliths in the journal Frontiers for Young Minds (Figure 1a)—an alternative academic journal that includes children as peer reviewers and input from mentors and teachers. This paper provides an accessible introduction to rhodoliths, targeted towards younger audiences (but with relevance for all ages), engaging participation in the scientific process from a young age and inspiring the next generation of scientists. Key concepts and terminologies were introduced, such as the importance and implications of their global distribution, with local context of searching for ‘redstones’ or ‘rhodoliths’ at the beach, how coralline algae act as ‘bioengineers’, their role in ‘blue carbon’ and the importance of conserving these unique habitats for future prosperity. We suggest that through increasing the number of popular science articles about coralline algal research, it is possible to bolster the public understanding of the occurrence and global significance of coralline algal habitats. Encouraging widespread distribution of these articles via social media and other outlets will enable coordinated global scale reach. Marine science documentaries can inspire, educate and transform complex scientific concepts and serve to be informative tools in science communication (Staaterman et al. 2014). Biogenic maerl beaches are geologically significant, with rhodoliths found frequently in Cenozoic deposits and through the formation of recent sedimentological micro-habitats such as maerl subaqueous dunes (mega-ripples) as well as biogenic gravel beaches composed of fossilised maerl debris (Freiwald 1995; Joshi 2016). Therefore, they have proven to be a useful example to illustrate Principle 2—where biology and geology meet. The Trá an Doílín beach in county Galway, Ireland, is popularly known as the ‘Coral Strand’ in the local community and frequently mistaken to be composed of coral, not coralline algae. To elevate coralline algae ocean literacy and to preserve local knowledge previously unrecorded, Dr Siddhi Joshi led the direction and production of an hour-long documentary ‘Maerl: A Rare Seabed Habitat’. In this, focusing on Ireland's maerl beds, the documentary covered a diverse range of multidisciplinary maerl research spanning botany, zoology, habitat mapping, conservation management, geology, hydrographic surveying and geophysics (Joshi and Documentary Team 2014; Figure 1b). Through a series of interviews with coralline algae/maerl experts, this documentary was able to show how stakeholders and coastal communities are affected by anthropogenic activities that target maerl bed, including commercial extraction, dredging, salmon farming and bottom trawling, now freely available on the ‘Seabed Habitats’ collaborative research blog (seabedhabitats.org). Through a film screening on Aran Island in county Galway, west of Ireland (at Inisheer Film Festival), the film helped raise local awareness of the presence and conservation significance of maerl, in light of a previous salmon farm proposal in a neighbouring local area. Attendees included local fishermen and conservation groups, tourists and marine scientists. Another locally significant maerl documentary is ‘Pink Maerl and Salmon Farms– Scotland's Barrier Reef’ (Little Green Island Films 2023), inspired by the rising citizen science movement to protect maerl beds in Scotland (Hall-Spencer and Rasmusson 2024; Figure 1c). Receiving more than 2000 views from around the world, these video documentaries have given regional coralline algal science a global public platform. Biogeochemistry can be easily left behind in public engagement by not being as charismatic or broadly understood as other topics like biodiversity (Sutton et al. 2013). However, their known importance as a globally important pool of dimethylated sulphur (Burdett, Hatton, and Kamenos 2015) has been showcased in public settings, including in a headline talk by Dr Heidi Burdett at the 2019 New Scientist Live—one of the largest science festivals in the world with more than 40,000 attendees, mostly of school age (Figure 1d). Dimethylated sulphur is important for weather and climate because of the role it may play in local-regional climate regulation through the stimulation of cloud formation (Hopkins et al. 2023). The longevity of coralline algae and the complexity of their calcium carbonate skeleton means they are excellent recorders of past climate, allowing us to reconstruct Holocene environmental change and identify the impacts of human activity. Research led by Dr Branwen Williams on Arctic coralline climate reconstruction has been featured by National Geographic, with a video on YouTube (link here) that has received 46,000 views from around the world (Figure 1e; Williams, personal communication). Marine photosynthesis supports almost all ocean life, and coralline algae have been recorded at deeper depths than any other alga in the ocean (Gattuso et al. 2006). The extraordinary ability for coralline algae to photosynthesise under a huge range of light environments has been a recent research focus (e.g., Krieger et al. 2023; Voerman et al. 2023; Voerman et al. 2022). However, photosynthesis—especially in the ocean—can be an abstract concept to understand. Since 2019, Dr Heidi Burdett in collaboration with Dekko Comics (dekkocomics.com) has used science comics as a medium to start conversations and increase knowledge about coralline algal photosynthesis. Drawing on the known benefits of science comics with visual design, narratives and metaphors (Farinella 2018), more than 6000 copies of this comic have been distributed around the world (as of May 2024) in schools, museums, music festivals, and more (Figure 1f). Known to increase engagement, learning motivation and improve understanding of STEM subjects, science comics are a valuable but underexplored genre in science communication. Scientific illustrations have also been suggested by rhodolith researchers in Costa Rica to help communicate the importance of rhodoliths to different age groups and to rangers of marine protected areas (MPAs), so they can identify rhodoliths in their conservation areas. The group is actively involved in talks to university students, courses for the nonscientific population about algae where this topic is included, as well as education for tourism companies so they can recognise the beds and the ecosystem services they provide (Fernández García, personal communication). Coralline algal habitats are one of the ocean's most biodiverse ecosystems (Tuya et al. 2023). They therefore present an opportunity to showcase Nature's biodiversity beyond the more commonly targeted ecosystems such as coral reefs or rainforests. Art–science collaboration, such as Julia Barton's maerl-themed ‘Littoral Art Project’ and performance exhibition (littoralartproject.com), is one novel example which utilised dance as an art-form to communicate the diversity of coralline algae ecosystems. Using personifications of a crab, sea cucumber and octopus to artistically explore the maerl habitat, the threats of anthropogenic activities such as dredging and the ‘sanctuary’ can be provided by MPAs. Marine citizen science is a growing method for improving ocean literacy and has now reached out to coralline algae. In Scotland, for example, citizen science initiatives via Seasearch (www.seasearch.org.uk) have helped to increase biodiversity records on maerl beds and improve conservation policy compliance (Hall-Spencer and Rasmusson 2024). Maerl is a celebrated commodity in some parts of the world. For example, the ‘Fête du maerl’ cultural festival takes place every 4 years in Brittany with more than 5000 attendees. Historically, maerl was used as a soil conditioner for treating acidic soils in the region; extraction of maerl from the seabed therefore had the dual benefit of supporting fertile agricultural land and a fishing industry (Figure 1g). Over time then, maerl has had an inextricable link to human socioeconomic and cultural values that continues to live on in conservation initiatives and museum exhibitions (e.g., Óceanopolis aquarium in Brest; www.oceanopolis.com/en). At a global scale, coralline algae have also been a leading feature of local initiatives around the world within the Global Seaweed-SUPERSTAR Project (https://globalseaweed.org/). For example, the project has capacity-building initiatives in Tanzania, Malaysia and the Philippines to improve seaweed (including coralline algae) taxonomic identification skills in the local community and to identify diseases. These efforts are communicated via policy briefs and guidance videos for publics and industry. Despite their ecological, biogeochemical, socioeconomic and cultural importance and global ubiquity, coralline algae and their associated habitats remain poorly studied (Tuya et al. 2023). Even now, our knowledge about their distribution remains patchy. Public engagement with coralline algae citizen science initiatives hold promise for scaling-up our exploration and distributional recording of coralline algae habitats (Hall-Spencer and Rasmusson 2024). This can be further facilitated by public networks such as the Irish Ocean Literacy Network (irishoceanliteracy.ie) and combined ocean mapping-outreach initiatives such as the ‘Real Map of Ireland’—which showcases the tremendous progress we have made in recent years to map our ocean floors and identify new habitats such as maerl beds. Through participation in international conferences (COLACMAR, SOFILAC), data on rhodolith beds in Costa Rica have been translated into maps showing conservation gaps at the national level. This helps to additionally augment the research efforts studying fossil and recent rhodoliths from Central America (Caribbean and Pacific), with the aim of identifying the main species (from a morphological and genetic perspective), analysing their historical biogeography, describing their development environments and generating conservation recommendations (Fernández García, personal communication). Embracing emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence is crucial if coralline algae science is to keep pace with other disciplines. It is here where innovation in coralline algal public engagement can build on its foundation, looking forward to our next generation of coralline algal scientists, conservationists, policymakers and advocates. A first step towards this can be seen in the NASA NeMO-Net videogame (van den Bergh et al. 2021; nemonet.info; Figure 1h) where seabed classification (of, for example, coral reefs and coralline algae) feeds into a neural network to progress and improve habitat mapping. In this citizen science for the digital age, users are trained in seabed classification on virtual dives by a Remotely Operated Vehicle. Having reached more than 300 million people within 7 months of launch (van den Bergh et al. 2021), it is clear that digital engagement holds great potential for widespread engagement in both ocean and technology literacy. We hope to see more innovations in this space with particular emphasis on co-creating with educators' materials, resources and information. Most people only engage with the ocean through the visual of the flat ocean surface, suggesting calm and nothingness underneath (Williams, personal communication). Visualising the ocean as a three-dimensional or more dynamic four-dimensional changing environment is therefore of crucial importance and may be achieved using emerging technologies such as ocean digital twins—a current multimillion Euro priority for the European Commission (European Commission 2022). Activities that highlight how complex the ocean is and integral to our earth science is important. This is done particularly well through engaging storytelling, including high-quality visuals—several examples of which we have highlighted here. Language and illiteracy barriers should be overcome through translation of resources to local language(s) and a focus on non–text-based engagement materials. Coralline algae-specific resources are sparse compared to other habitats and species—here we demonstrate that redressing this balance can help us to advance local–global ocean literacy. Permissions are granted for the use of images in Figure 1, where necessary. The authors would like to thank Cindy Fernández García and Branwen Williams for their contributions to the global examples included for the ocean literacy principles and two anonymous reviewers for improving the quality of the final manuscript. The authors have nothing to report. The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Not applicable.
