AB  - <title>Abstract</title>
                <p>
                  According to IFRS, IAS 41 requires companies to measure and report biological assets at fair value. This study examines a sample of Norwegian fish farming companies to assess whether measuring salmon at fair value provides greater value relevance than measuring it at cost. Our analysis begins with a survey of financial analysts' practices, which reveals that analysts generally prefer cost over fair value when forecasting cash flows.
We then empirically evaluate the value relevance of fair value relative to cost. The findings, consistent with analysts' views, indicate that cost-based reporting has higher overall value relevance. Although fair value measurement enhances the relevance of the balance sheet, it reduces earnings relevance.
Following regulatory criticism by Norway’s Financial Supervisory Authorities (FSA), the industry harmonized its fair value measurement practices for immature fish to comply with IFRS 13. This harmonization improved the relevance of fair value balance sheets. By the end of the sample period, the overall value relevance of fair value is as significant as cost.
                  <bold>JEL Codes:</bold>
                  M41, G31, Q22
                </p>
