AB  - <jats:p>Lice-eating cleaner fish (lumpfish, Cyclopterus lumpus; and wrasses, Labridae) are used as a biological control measure of salmon lice (Lepeophtheirus salmonis) infestation in salmonid fish farms. In Norway, a delousing treatment is applied when the lice infestation level approaches the legal threshold. Though these treatments are effective in removing salmon lice, they have adverse side-effects on health, mortality and growth of the treated fish. Here, we analysed effects of using cleaner fish on salmonid production through reducing the number of necessary delousing treatments.We simulated salmon louse dynamics under conditions representative for salmonid farms in southern and central Norway without and with presence of wrasses or lumpfish. We compared low and high delousing-induced salmon mortality, and 70 % and 80 % delousing treatment success. Using wrasses at 5 % of the stocked salmon number or lumpfish at 10 % of the stocked number increased production and welfare of farmed salmon by reducing delousing treatments by about 10 %. The use of cleaner fish reduced the louse emission by the farm by about 6 %, thus positively affecting wild salmon welfare. An index for the welfare of salmon and cleaner fish combined was, however, not improved by use of cleaner fish.</jats:p>
