AB  - <jats:p>Recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS) commonly rely on moving-bed biofilm reactors that oxidise nitrogenous waste to nitrate, which accumulates and must be removed by water exchange. A microalgae-based loop could instead assimilate ammonia and phosphate into harvestable biomass, coupling treatment with resource recovery, but its behaviour for cold-water salmonids is little documented. We present a long-duration case study (Shiga, Japan) in which parallel single-tank RAS units were run and windows drawn for two microalgae-based rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) trials and one conventional control; being unreplicated, outcomes are reported descriptively to extract operational parameters, not to compare performance. Rainbow trout completed multi-month cycles (R1: 44 fish, 115 days, two mortalities; R2: 29 fish, 158 days, none; control: 90 fish, 205 days). Total ammonia in the microalgae units exceeded the 1.9 mg L⁻¹ standard during an extended start-up (peaking near 12 mg L⁻¹) but fell below it once pH recovered above 6.5 and a dense, growing culture was established, defining an operating window. Survival reflected low un-ionised ammonia at low pH; because low pH itself stresses fish, health endpoints need further study. Strong nitrate accumulation (to ~229 mg L⁻¹) showed an algae–bacteria consortium in which nitrification shared the nitrogen load. The study provides concrete design parameters — pre-maturing the consortium before stocking, controlling alkalinity from the outset, and closing the nitrogen budget — and a roadmap for resource-recovering microalgae-based RAS in cold-water aquaculture.</jats:p>
