AB  - <jats:p>Renibacterium salmoninarum is a facultative intracellular bacterium and the etiological agent of bacterial kidney disease (BKD), characterized by its ability to survive and persist within host cells, particularly macrophages, through multiple evasion mechanisms, including catalase production, reduction of superoxide anions, and low replication rates, which collectively allow it to evade cellular bactericidal responses. In this context, modulation of the autophagy–lysosomal pathway has emerged as a key survival strategy employed by several intracellular bacteria; however, whether R. salmoninarum can directly regulate this pathway in the host remains unclear. Autophagy is a highly conserved cellular process that contributes to the elimination of intracellular pathogens through their degradation within the lysosomal system, representing a fundamental component of innate immunity known as xenophagy. Nevertheless, numerous pathogens have evolved mechanisms to subvert this pathway in order to promote their intracellular survival and replication. In this study, we evaluated the modulation of the autophagy–lysosomal pathway during R. salmoninarum infection in SHK-1 cells and in the head kidney of Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar). Our results revealed an early upregulation of key autophagy-related genes, including Atg5, Atg12, Beclin, P62, Rab7, Uvrag, and Tfeb, in both experimental models, followed by a downregulation at later stages of infection. Regarding lysosomal function, R. salmoninarum induced an early increase in the expression of Lamp1, Lamp2, Cd63, and Cathepsin B in SHK-1 cells, whereas in the anterior kidney, only Lamp1 and Cathepsin B showed increased expression. At later stages, a progressive decrease in the expression of these genes was observed, accompanied by a differential expression pattern of lysosomal markers, with Lamp2 and Cd63 detected in SHK-1 cells but not in kidney tissue. Overall, these findings suggest that R. salmoninarum initially triggers a host defensive autophagy–lysosomal response but subsequently modulates or subverts these mechanisms to promote its intracellular persistence. This study provides new insights into host–pathogen interactions in BKD and highlights modulation of the autophagy–lysosomal pathway as a potential therapeutic target in salmon aquaculture.</jats:p>
