AB  - <jats:p>Average transaction prices collected by surveys, assessments or secondary data often serve as price benchmarks in markets that lack centralized exchanges. This paper examines when and how average prices facilitate eective price discovery. To do so we investigate a dynamic economic model that centers the coordinating role of the average price as a common reference price. We derive conditions for local price discovery and optimal price revision rates. The model provides sucient structure to estimate structural model parameters, including transaction price revision rates and implied market prot margins using only transaction price data. In a case study we estimate the model on transaction price data on Norwegian farmed salmon exports. We document frequent price revisions (average monthly rate of about 69%), signicant pricing agreement, and estimated buyer and seller prot margins consistent with industry data. The paper highlights the crucial role of Price Reporting Agencies (PRA) in determining the accuracy, eciency, and informativeness of price measures in decentralized markets.</jats:p>
