AB  - <jats:p>In recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS), off-flavors in fish have led to the requirement of a depuration process, which increases production costs, delays sales, and even causes unwanted reputation among customers in the case of unsuccessful depuration. Many different methods have been studied and tested to prevent and remove the unwanted flavors formed in RAS, but still, a sufficiently effective method has not been found to replace depuration. Here, we studied cyclodextrin- and chitosan-modified viscose nonwoven materials and their ability to capture off-flavor compounds, especially geosmin (GSM). GSM belongs to a group of terpenoid compounds, typically inducing unwanted musty and earthy flavor to fish. The viscose-based capturing material was introduced in a side-loop of a RAS rearing rainbow trout, Oncorhynchus mykiss. The experiment was continued for five weeks, and concentrations of 14 selected off-flavor compounds, including GSM, were measured in water, in fish muscle, and in the functionalized viscose nonwoven. The concentrations of GSM and 2-methyl isoborneol (MIB) decreased below the known sensory thresholds. For the other studied compounds, the concentrations similarly decreased, although their sensory thresholds in fish are unknown. The results suggest that the viscose-based capturing material in RAS was effective in reducing off-flavors from the recirculating water and preventing their accumulation in fish flesh up to detectable amounts by human senses.</jats:p>
