AB  - <jats:p>Atlantic salmon aquaculture is a rapidly expanding global industry, contributing significantly the economies of major producing countries over the past decade. However, it experiences continuous challenges including a 20-30% mortality rate due to both infectious and non-infectious disease and other factors. Farmed salmon experience stressors throughout their growth including handling, transportation, and exposure to a diversity of pathogens, which triggers the release of cortisol. Cortisol binds to the glucocorticoid (GR) and mineralocorticoid receptor (MR), leading to impacts on the immune system. The genome of the Atlantic salmon has experienced recent whole genome duplication resulting in multiple corticosteroid receptor paralogues: four GR and two MR. Currently, it is not known whether cortisol-mediated immune modulation is driven in a paralogue specific manner. To investigate this, we stimulated Atlantic salmon with a viral mimic (poly I:C) or a heat-killed Vibrio anguillarum for 24 hours to induce a strong antiviral and/or proinflammatory response. Gene expression analysis suggest GR and MR paralogues may have different functionality within the crosstalk of the immune-neuroendocrine system. We found that that GR and MR paralogue-specific expression changes were tissue type (with a maximum response in spleen) and immune stimulant dependent, suggesting that paralogous GR and MR genes may have distinct roles within the crosstalk between stress and immune responses. These results further our understanding of the impacts of stress on the immune function of Atlantic salmon and help inform industry with regards to improvement of fish health and welfare.</jats:p>
