AB  - Pacific salmon and trout play vital roles in the culture of Indigenous Peoples, in commercial, recreational, and subsistence fisheries and aquaculture, and as icons of the environment around the North Pacific Rim. They return from the sea to spawn and die in streams and lakes, enriching freshwater and riparian ecosystems, so their ecological role is also great. This paper reviews some of the key themes in salmon conservation research in the 1970s (especially logging, hydroelectric dams, fisheries interceptions, and hatchery production) and considers to what extent those issues have been resolved or remain pressing. The paper then considers some of the many topics that were of less concern five decades ago such as net pen aquaculture, chemical contaminants, effects of invasive species, the ecology of transplanted salmon populations, and others. These topics reveal changing conservation themes in fisheries science and the social systems in which science is practiced. The goal is to alert readers to the surprises that the future may bring and encourage optimism that salmon will persist.
