AB  - <jats:p>Structural variations (SVs) are crucial for phenotypic diversity and adaptive evolution in organisms. Recent studies revealed the divergence in salinity tolerance ability of landlocked rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss), suggesting the potential for offshore aquaculture of salinity-tolerant strains. However, the genetic basis underlying salinity tolerance remained largely unexplored. Here, we constructed a comprehensive landscape of SVs by whole-genome re-sequencing of 25 tolerant and 25 sensitive rainbow trout individuals. A total of 151,315 high-confidence SVs were identified, with an uneven chromosomal distribution and 465 hotspot regions spanning ~ 326 Mb. Genetic structure analysis revealed no genetic differences between tolerant and sensitive individuals. Based on the high-confidence SV dataset, FST method identified 155 genomic regions under signature selection between tolerant and sensitive individuals. Additionally, GWAS for salinity tolerance further identified 1 significant locus: a DEL in the intergenic region of nsfl1c on chr16. Functional genes impacted by SVs were significantly enriched in GO terms and KEGG pathways related to cytoskeletal dynamics, intercellular communication and stress-signaling. Based on the RNA-seq datasets, 44 functional genes around candidate SVs showed the significantly differential expression between tolerant and sensitive individuals, including adcy9, antiprot1, itih5, and thsd4. Functional analyses revealed that the 44 genes were mainly implicated in 2 central mechanisms for salinity adaptation, including osmosensation and osmoregulation. Collectively, our results provided a comprehensive landscape of SVs and offered the genetic basis for divergence of salinity tolerance in rainbow trout, which will be valuable for the development of salinity-tolerant strains and offshore aquaculture of landlocked rainbow trout.</jats:p>
