AB  - <jats:p>The effect of hydroxychalcone derivatives compared to a watersoluble analogue of vitamin E (trolox) on the rearing performance of juvenile rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) and the level of lipid peroxidation in the liver was investigated in vitro and in vivo. One hundred percent survival was established in all experimental groups, with rearing performance not differing significantly from the control (p &gt; 0.05), while the Fulton’s condition factor reached 3.30 in the presence of the studied compounds, indicating good physiological condition of the fish. No behavioral deviations were
observed during the experiment, suggesting the absence of negative effects of the studied compounds. Fish receiving chalcone derivatives showed no signs of bacterial infection characteristic of the control group (tail deformation
and exophthalmia). A prolonged (72-hour liver homogenate incubation) antioxidant effect was revealed for the chalcone with a sterically hindered phenol fragment both under in vitro liver lipid autoxidation conditions and upon promotion of the process with PbCl2, in contrast to the dihydroxychalcone derivative, which exhibited an inversion of antioxidant
properties to prooxidant effects. In in vivo experiments for this chalcone, a significant reduction in the level of TBARS in the liver by 30% and inhibition of non-enzymatic lipid peroxidation by 13% relative to the control were also
recorded. The results obtained from in vitro and in vivo experiments indicate the need for further research to enable the potential use of this chalcone derivative as a protective feed additive in artificial fish farming.</jats:p>
AU  - Polovinkina, M.A.
