AB  - The killer whale (Orcinus orca) is a polymorphic species that displays remarkable behavioral and ecological plasticity, adapting to local environmental conditions. In some regions, this adaptability has led to the emergence of sympatric ecotypes—distinct forms that coexist in the same geographic area but exhibit different ecological specializations. Despite often inhabiting the same regions, resident and Bigg's killer whales do not intermix or interbreed (Hoelzel and Dover 1991; Hoelzel et al. 1998; Barrett-Lennard 2000; Hoelzel et al. 2007; Morin et al. 2010; Parsons et al. 2013; Filatova et al. 2015; Foote et al. 2016). They differ significantly in morphology (Baird and Stacey 1988; Kotik et al. 2023), behavior (Morton 1990), vocal communication (Deecke et al. 2005), social organization (Baird and Dill 1995), and other traits. Due to these profound differences, the fish-eating and mammal-eating killer whale populations in the North Pacific have recently been recognized as separate subspecies: O. orca ater and O. orca rectipinnus, respectively (Morin et al. 2024). One of the most striking differences between these two ecotypes/subspecies is their social structure. Resident killer whales exhibit a unique social system unlike that of any other mammalian species: both male and female offspring remain in their natal group for their entire lives (Ford et al. 2000). As a result, they form large matrilineal groups that can span up to four generations, all descended from a single female. Bigg's killer whales form smaller and more fluid social groups, where postnatal dispersal is common (Ford and Ellis 1999; Baird and Dill 1995; Nielsen et al. 2023). This has traditionally been attributed to their specialized foraging strategy (Baird and Dill 1995). Unlike resident killer whales, which feed primarily on fish, Bigg's killer whales prey mainly on marine mammals such as seals, sea lions, and other cetaceans—a diet that demands stealth and coordinated hunting tactics. Smaller group sizes are thought to reduce the likelihood of detection by prey, enhancing hunting efficiency. As a result, their social structure is more flexible, with individuals often leaving their natal matriline and forming temporary associations driven by hunting needs rather than stable family ties. This concept was further supported by observations in the North Atlantic, where mammal-hunting killer whales were seen in smaller groups compared to members of the same population that were feeding on fish (Deecke et al. 2011; Beck et al. 2012). Interestingly, most hypotheses addressing the differences in social structure between resident and Bigg's killer whales have focused on why Bigg's disperse from their maternal groups after birth, rather than on why residents do not—despite the fact that the social structure of Bigg's killer whales is more common, while the residents' structure is highly unusual. Some studies have highlighted the benefits of the residents' stable social system, such as kin-directed altruism (e.g., food sharing with adult offspring; Wright et al. 2016). However, they have also noted certain costs, including reduced survival of adult offspring following the death of their mother (Foster et al. 2012) or grandmother (Nattrass et al. 2019). Yet, the question of why residents have evolved such an extremely stable social structure in the first place has received little serious attention. This social structure does not appear to offer significant advantages in foraging, as resident killer whales typically hunt their primary prey—salmon (Oncorhynchus sp.)—individually. One reason why animals form large groups is for protection against predators (Caro 2005). Killer whales have long been considered to have no natural predators. The only potential predator for a killer whale is another killer whale, but reports of cannibalism are extremely rare. The only documented evidence dates back to the whaling era, when the remains of killer whales were found in the stomachs of two individuals from the same group, observed in the warm waters of the Southern Hemisphere between 30° and 50° southern latitude (Shevchenko 1975). Direct interactions between resident and Bigg's killer whales are rarely observed. Bigg's killer whales appear to actively avoid residents (Baird 2000; our unpublished data), and in one well-documented case, were even aggressively attacked by them (Ford and Ellis 1999). Additionally, Towers et al. (2018) documented a fatal attack by Bigg's killer whales on a neonate calf from another Bigg's killer whale group, despite attempts by the calf's mother and other group members to protect it. Although there was no evidence of consumption in that case, the event demonstrates that Bigg's killer whales are capable of attacking another killer whale group and killing one of its members despite the potential risks involved. The only recent evidence of possible cannibalism in killer whales comes from Fomin (2022), who found a torn-off killer whale dorsal fin with killer whale tooth marks, although that case lacked genetic data to confirm the ecotype of the prey. In this study, we present direct evidence of conspecific predation on resident killer whales and discuss the potential implications and drivers related to their social structure. We report the discovery of remains from two resident killer whales bearing distinctive killer whale tooth marks, found on separate occasions on Bering Island (Commander Islands, Russia). The first remains were discovered on August 1, 2022, and were described in detail by Fomin (2022). They consisted of a dorsal fin with surrounding tissue, likely from a juvenile killer whale, measuring 47 cm in height (Figure 1A). The second remains were found on July 31, 2024, approximately 2 km north of the original site. This specimen also included a dorsal fin, but it was larger, measuring 71 cm in height—likely from an adult female or a juvenile male (Figure 1B). Both specimens bore distinctive fresh scars consistent in size and spacing with typical killer whale tooth marks (Figure 1C–E). Ecotype identification of the remains was performed using the sequences of the control region of the mitochondrial DNA which are distinct between resident and Bigg's killer whales. Skin samples from the remains were placed in 96% ethanol for storage and transportation. DNA was extracted using DNeasy Blood and Tissue kit (Qiagen, Valenica, CA) according to the protocol provided by the manufacturers. The mitochondrial control region was amplified using primer pairs L15926 (O'Corry-Crowe et al. 1997) + beluga_R1int (Meschersky et al. 2018) and beluga_intF2 [5′-AAATCACATAATATGCATGCTC-3′] + MTCRr (Hoelzel and Green 1998) were used with the following PCR amplification conditions: (1) initial denaturation at 95°C for 3 min; (2) denaturation at 94°C for 1 min; (3) primer annealing at 48°C for 1 min 30 s; (4) extension at 72°C for 1 min, 2–4 stages 30 cycles; (5) final extension at 72°C for 7 min; (6) hold at 15°C for 5 min. The primers delimited two overlapping mtDNA sections 416 and 734 bp long. The PCR products were sent for sequencing to Eurofins Genomics using their Mix2Sec Kit. Genetic analysis of both samples revealed that the mitochondrial control region haplotype was identical to SR haplotype (Genbank accession number DQ399077) typical for resident killer whales in this region (Parsons et al. 2013). These findings suggest that resident killer whales may occasionally be preyed upon by sympatric mammal-eating Bigg's killer whales. We have to admit, however, that scavenging on the carcasses of dead resident killer whales cannot be definitively ruled out. Killer whales are known to exploit carrion, including feeding on conspecifics killed by whalers during the commercial whaling era (Shevchenko 1975). Consequently, the consumption of resident individuals by Bigg's killer whales could, in principle, reflect opportunistic scavenging rather than active predation. However, freshly dead killer whale carcasses typically sink rapidly, making them inaccessible for consumption, which suggests that predation remains a plausible explanation. Cannibalism is common among some apex predators (Steyn and Funston 2006; Ryazanov et al. 2018; Ivanov et al. 2020; Ito et al. 2022); besides, resident and Bigg's killer whales do not interbreed or socially interact. Consequently, interactions between these ecotypes may be more appropriately interpreted as interspecific predation rather than cannibalism from a biological and behavioral perspective. If Bigg's killer whales indeed sometimes kill and consume conspecifics, the unique social structure of resident killer whales, characterized by large, cohesive groups of closely related individuals, may serve as an antipredator adaptation. High relatedness within these groups likely promotes mutual defense, with individuals more inclined to protect one another due to kin selection, which supports altruistic behavior among relatives. This may explain observations of large resident groups chasing away smaller groups of Bigg's killer whales (Ford and Ellis 1999), as well as why Bigg's killer whales tend to avoid large resident groups when they detect their vocalizations from a distance (Baird 2000; our unpublished data). The only other toothed whale species known to successfully confront and even drive away killer whales is the long-finned pilot whale (Globicephala melas) (De Stephanis et al. 2015; Selbmann et al. 2022), which is largely attributed to their highly social nature. Pilot whales typically form large herds of several dozens to hundreds of individuals, structured around matrilineal kinship, with little to no postnatal dispersal in either sex (Amos et al. 1993), similar to the social organization of resident killer whales. Observations in the North Atlantic have documented pilot whale herds chasing away smaller groups of killer whales (De Stephanis et al. 2015; Selbmann et al. 2022). Furthermore, playback experiments using killer whale vocalizations have triggered a marked increase in pilot whale group cohesion and a strong attraction toward the sound source (Curé et al. 2012). However, there are also published observations of killer whales chasing a pod of 15 pilot whales in Peruvian waters (García-Godos 2004); in this case, the pilot whales involved were most likely short-finned pilot whales (Globicephala macrorhynchus). These contrasting observations suggest that the relationship between killer whales and pilot whales is likely complex and context dependent, encompassing both antagonistic and defensive interactions. While pilot whales may engage in collective mobbing behavior that can deter or drive away killer whales, particularly when present in large, cohesive groups, killer whales are also capable of pursuing and potentially preying upon pilot whales under certain ecological or social conditions. Most other toothed whale species live in fission–fusion societies, where group membership is fluid and individuals in a group are mostly unrelated (except for mother-calf pairs). As a result, there is little evolutionary incentive for group members to protect one another. Even when large herds of dolphins, sometimes numbering in the thousands, encounter a small group of killer whales (or even a single individual), they do not mount a collective defense. Instead, they attempt to flee individually, each animal fighting for its own survival. Another line of evidence supporting our hypothesis is that, in many regions where no sympatric mammal-eating ecotype exists, few killer whale populations have been shown to exhibit the lifelong natal philopatry characteristic of the North Pacific residents, even among populations with similar fish-based diets (Ford 2019). For instance, Tavares et al. (2017) found that Icelandic herring-eating killer whales display a multilevel social structure without clear hierarchical tiers or stable, nested social units. While associations were nonrandom, strong, and lasting bonds were relatively rare, unlike the cohesive, stable groups of North Pacific residents. In Norwegian killer whales, maternal kinship explained the strongest associations, but many nonkin associations also occurred (Jourdain et al. 2024). In the Strait of Gibraltar, killer whales feeding on migrating bluefin tuna (Thunnus thynnus) form highly stable matrilineal groups (Esteban et al. 2016), although the extent of dispersal within these groups remains unclear. Less is known about killer whale social structures in the Southern Hemisphere. Our hypothesis is consistent with observations from Antarctic killer whale communities, where mean group sizes for known (Ross Sea) and suspected (Gerlache) fish-eating ecotypes are larger than for mammal-eating (type A and pack ice) forms (Pitman and Ensor 2003; Durban et al. 2016). In areas with no sympatric fish- and mammal-eating ecotypes described, the data are less clear. A preliminary study off the Brazilian coast suggested that killer whales may form stable social units that also exhibit some degree of fission-fusion dynamics (Athayde et al. 2023). At subantarctic Marion Island, Tosh et al. (2008) observed nonrandom patterns of association and dissociation, indicating a distinctive social structure that combines traits of both Northern Hemisphere resident and Bigg's killer whales. Jordaan et al. (2021) reported seasonal changes in social structure at Marion Island, with fusion during periods of high prey abundance and fission during periods of low prey abundance. In the Galápagos Islands, Denkinger et al. (2020) used social network analysis to identify three distinct social units and one loosely connected group with frequent interchange among individuals, suggesting a fluid, fission-fusion-like social organization. At Crozet Island, stable social units included both related and unrelated whales, suggesting some level of dispersal from matrilines (Delaire et al. 2026). In summary, the highly stable, matrilineal social structure of resident killer whales in the North Pacific stands out as a rare and evolutionarily puzzling phenomenon among mammalian species. While past explanations have focused on foraging strategies and kin-directed benefits, our findings suggest that antipredator defense against sympatric, mammal-eating Bigg's killer whales may have played a significant role in shaping this unique social system. The discovery of cannibalized resident individuals provides rare direct evidence of intraspecific predation, highlighting the potential for lethal interecotype interactions. Conversely, in ecosystems where no such predation pressure exists, killer whale populations tend to exhibit more flexible social structures with variable levels of kinship. Together, these patterns suggest that the evolution of lifelong natal philopatry in resident killer whales may be driven not only by ecological specialization but also by the need for collective defense, making their social organization a remarkable example of how predator–prey dynamics can shape complex social behavior in marine mammals. Olga A. Filatova: conceptualization, funding acquisition, writing – original draft, writing – review and editing. Ivan D. Fedutin: methodology; validation; investigation; Sergey V. Fomin: methodology; investigation; resources. This work was supported by Human Frontier Science Program (RGP0045/2022). The authors declare no conflicts of interest. The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.
