AB  - Fish culture, like all animal production systems, generates waste. In aquaculture, waste is broadly classified into solid and dissolved waste. The major source of solid waste is undigested feed, excreted as faeces. Removal success of faeces from production system depends on characteristics like size, stability and density of faecal pellets. Vast amount of knowledge is available on factors determining faecal quantity in various fish species. Therefore, this thesis focused on the less explored area of faecal quality. The impact of the dietary (ingredient and nutrient composition of diet), environmental (salinity) and fish-specific (species) factors in determining faecal quality were investigated and as a secondary aim the impact on faecal quantity was taken along. Salmonids were selected as the model group due to their high economic importance, widespread culture in RAS and euryhaline nature. Since there were no standard methods available for determining faecal stability, a simplified and standardized protocol for measuring faecal stability was developed and used across different chapters of this thesis. First, the impact of a diverse set of ingredients (incl. novel) was evaluated regarding the quantity and quality of faecal waste produced by rainbow trout (Oncorhnychus mykiss). Results showed that ingredients in the diet determine the amount of faecal waste produced, sedimentation based removal efficiency, and the amount of non-removed faeces accumulating in the system. Findings suggested that the high dietary carbohydrate content impaired faecal quality, whereas high dietary ash content improved it. Building on these leads, the impact of dietary starch levels and dietary ash content on faecal quality in rainbow trout was investigated. It was also evaluated if exogenous supplementation of amylase could mitigate the potential adverse impacts of starch on faecal quality. Elevated starch levels decreased faecal removal efficiency by sedimentation and increased the quantity of non-removed faeces. Amylase supplementation improved starch digestibility, but did not alter the faecal waste production, removal efficiency, size, and stability of faeces. As hypothesized, increasing dietary ash enhanced faeces' density and sinking velocity but did not result in a measurable improvement in faecal removal efficiency. A species comparison trial revealed that even closely related salmonids produce faeces with markedly different sizes and stability, even when fed the same diet and reared under the same conditions (same systems). Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus) produced faeces dominated by particles of smaller size and lower stability compared to rainbow trout and Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar). No consistent trends were observed regarding how salinity affected faecal size and stability. Finally, I demonstrated that rainbow trout reared in saline water produce faeces of diverse types and that faecal pellets have a dual mucoidal coating. It was also shown that coating is an important factor determining the faeces size and stability. A generalized conceptual framework integrating these findings was developed and system design and water flow management were identified as critical factors determining the efficacy of dietary interventions in determining faecal quality. To conclude this thesis demonstrates that, faecal quality is governed by complex interplay of dietary, environmental, species-specific, and system-level factors.
