AB  - <jats:p>Production cost in Norwegian salmon farming roughly doubled between 2016 and 2024, reversing two decades of decline, and the standard explanation is a ``biological treadmill&amp;apos;&amp;apos; of sea lice and disease. Using firm-level data from the Norwegian Directorate of Fisheries&amp;apos; profitability survey, I decompose the increase and ask whether biology raises cost through damage or through prevention. Two engines dominate. Global feed-price inflation accounts for about a third of the rise, with the feed-conversion ratio almost unchanged. The cost of managing biological risk accounts for at least a fifth, and it works through prevention rather than damage: a shift to post-smolt---juveniles reared nearly twice as large on land, at roughly triple the cost per fish, to shorten the lice-exposed sea phase---while mortality, feed conversion and harvest weight barely move. A stochastic cost frontier confirms the reading: returns to scale are mild, technical regress is modest, and firm-level inefficiency is small and stable. The cost of biological risk in the accounts is preventive investment, visible in the smolt line rather than in fish-health costs or a collapse in productivity.​</jats:p>
