AB  - As the global population continues to grow, there is an increased global demand for protein, especially fish. According to the United Nations (UN), sustainable fisheries and aquaculture will be essential to combatting world hunger. The increased production of seafood has led to an abundance of discarded by-products (e.g., heads, fins, scales, bones, blood, viscera) that are disposed of at sea or in biohazard landfills. However, the byproducts have the potential to act as a feedstock for various materials, such as collagen and oils. Most studies that investigate the potential of valorizing fishery discards often ignore the inorganic component of the by-products. The aim of this thesis was to design an optimized treatment based on the 12 principles of green chemistry (Chapter 1) to isolate hydroxyapatite (HAP) from Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) processing wastes and transform the raw material to several value-added products including nanoparticles and carbon dots (CDs). Most existing processes to isolate HAP from fish by-products are too hazardous and/or energy intensive for industrial application, and this is discussed in Chapter 2. Using enzymes offers a sustainable solution to previously reported methods by lowering the energy required and eliminating the use of concentrated acids and/or bases. In Chapter 3, an enzymatic treatment using a protease and lipase simultaneously was optimized for use in tap water to isolate HAP from Atlantic salmon wastes (sHAP). This technology was successfully tested on a 20 L scale, and a rudimentary cost assessment was performed. The cleaned fish bones were transformed to nanoparticles (nHAP) in Chapter 4 using a combination of ball milling and ultrasound (US) in aqueous organic acids. Depending on the choice of organic acid, the size of nHAP can be tailored, and particles prepared with 10% propanoic acid for 15 min had an average diameter of 28.7 nm. For the enzymatic and nanoparticle processes, separate life cycle analyses (LCA) were completed, each demonstrating that the methods were much greener compared to other publications. For example, the method to create nHAP based on US had 97% less CO2 emissions than other non-hazardous processes. Beyond nHAP, preliminary studies suggest other materials can be created using sHAP. In Chapter 5, CDs were synthesized by microwave (MW) irradiation of lemon juice (LJ), and it was observed that these CDs could be doped with Ca, P, and N by addition of sHAP during the reaction. sHAP has also shown promise as a material to create composites by mixing it with collagen, and there is potential in using it for dental applications and as a sustainable fertilizer (Chapter 6).
