AB  - This paper estimates the effect of spatial proximity to other salmon farms on aquaculture firms’ costs. Open cage salmon aquaculture farms cause and face externalities, such as various diseases and salmon lice. Hydrodynamic forces and the spatial density of farms influence the transmission of these externalities. We analyze the effects of salmon farm proximity on production cost using a translog cost function estimated from a panel data set of Norwegian aquaculture farms. We employ two indicators of the spatial density of aquaculture production. The first indicator is the mean distance to the nearest farm, and the second indicator is the number of farms within 20 km distance for all the farms. We find that increased proximity to other farms is associated with significant cost increase. The results imply that there are economic welfare effects due to the farm location structure and the spatial biological externalities between farms.
