AB  - Tetracapsuloides bryosalmonae is a significant member of Malacosporea, the less speciose class of the cnidarian subphylum Myxozoa Grasse, 1970. This freshwater endoparasite is transmitted via malacospores from bryozoans and causes proliferative kidney disease in salmonid fishes. Due to the highly temperature-sensitive development of disease and transmission dynamics, T. bryosalmonae continues to be an emerging threat to both wild and farmed salmonid populations in the northern hemisphere. The parasite can persist and disperse through its propagation in clonal bryozoans that fragment and travel downstream as resistant statoblasts that can attach to migratory birds, e.g. Anas species, that traverse continents. Unique among myxozoans, T. bryosalmonae infection can induce at least partial immunity and thereby reduce the severity of reinfection and enhance survival in endemic environments.
