AB  - The introduction of new species of fishes to a geographical area or aquatic ecosystem, which are not native to them, from some other geographical area or aquatic ecosystem has been a very old practice (Welcome, 1988; Shetty et al., 1989; Sugunan, 1995). These non-native species once introduced to the new environment are referred as exotic or alien (Root et al., 2013). The non-native species have been introduced mainly for food purpose, sports, and aquarium trade (Padilla and Williams, 2004; De Silva et al., 2006). A few of the common fishes introduced in India for food purpose are Common carp, Cyprinus carpio; Grass carp, Ctenopharyngodon Idella; Silver carp, Hypophthalmichthys molitrix; Big head cap Aristicthys nobilis; Rainbow trout, Oncorhynchus mykiss; Tilapia, Oreochromis mossambicus; African catfish, Clarias gariepinus; Roop Chanda (Pacu), Piaractus brachypomus; Pangasius, Pangasiandon hypophthalmus; and many others (Bijukumar, 2000; Singh et al., 2013, 2014; Sandilyan, 2016). A few of these species were primarily introduced in aquaculture system and immensely helped to increase the fish production but somehow, some of these escaped into the open water bodies, established a breeding population, and gradually dominated over the native species to the extent of extinction of the latter as in case of Hypophthalmicthys molitrix in Gobindsagar reservoir in Himachal Pradesh, India (Kaushal, 2007). Orechromis mossambicus was introduced in India in 1952 as food fish (Sugunan, 1995). It gradually became a dominant species in large numbers of reservoir adversely affecting the population of native species (Sugunan, 1995; Lakra et al., 2008). The presence of O. mossambicus is now reported from the rivers and streams of the North-East (NE) (Goswami et al., 2012) and the Western Ghats (WGs) (Krishnakumar et al., 2009) India. Both the NE and the WGs are known as two biodiversity hotspots of India (Gadgil, 2011; Kurup and Radhakrishnan, 2006; Raghavan et al., 2008a; Goswami et al., 2012). The presence of exotic fish species has been listed as one of the reasons of depleting fish biodiversity of the WGs (Jain et al., 2022).
