AB  - A global shortage of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) could mean an increase in chronic conditions related to inflammation. One researcher's decade-long examination of the omega-3 shortage calls attention to this environmental and food justice issue that may quickly become a global health crisis. “Back when I was doing my postdoc, I found interesting molecular patterns that suggested some poor fetal growth might be related to deficiencies in omega-3s,” said Timothy Ciesielski, ScD, MD, MPH, Research Scientist at Case Western University's Swetland Center for Environmental Health. “Over the years, I've followed that up—not as my primary, core, ‘I'm paid to do this’ work, but I kept going with it, following it forward, because I felt like it was important.” In a recent interview with Oncology Times, Ciesielski explained that when evidence from recent metanalyses is combined with global dietary patterns and ecological data, it reveals that omega-3 can reduce the risk of several chronic diseases. However, we're not getting enough of it. In his call to action, Ciesielski expresses the need for greater access to affordable and uncontaminated omega-3s, sharing some insight into what patients can do for a more balanced diet. Research has shown that approximately 85 percent of the world's countries are not receiving enough omega-3s. While a naturally occurring nutrient, the human body cannot produce omega-3s to properly support cellular function and healthy human physiology. Although many fish and some plants are good sources of omega-3s, environmental and affordability factors limit accessibility. Coupled with imbalanced diets that are saturated in excess omega-6s, limited access to omega-3 sources can increase disease risk for heart disease, preterm birth, premenstrual symptom severity, wheezing, rheumatoid arthritis, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, neurocognitive decline and dementia, anxiety and depression, and certain cancers, including breast, prostate, and colon. “We're coming to the consensus that for many conditions you need a certain amount of omega-3,” Ciesielski said. “Many questions remain. [For example], if you need it, where are you going to get it? That is why I wanted to look at this—if you start to talk about where you're going to get it, you discover there's not enough in our food systems on average right now.” While access to and availability of omega-3 is difficult to come by, particularly in westernized diets, overconsuming food high in omega-6 is hard to avoid. Although the human body uses the same enzymes to process both fatty acids, omega-3 produces primarily anti-inflammatory compounds, while omega-6 produces predominantly pro-inflammatory molecules. Of the 184 countries studied, only 15 percent—most having access to extensive coastlines—are consuming enough omega-3s to balance the inflammatory effect of omega-6s. People should be encouraged to have conversations with their doctors on ways to integrate more omega-3 and reduce omega-6 in their diets. While both types of fat are essential, most people in Western food systems consume more than enough omega-6—often too much. However, Ciesielski offers insight on good foods to eat, particularly fish and other marine life low in contaminants, including some algae oils, kelp, and salmon. “Landlocked areas have a harder time getting the fish sources, and if they can get them, they're expensive,” Ciesielski said. “It's something we really need to think about, because right now, on a country level, we have rich countries fishing off the coasts of poorer countries, taking krill and fish, and bringing them back to rich countries, where they are making the rich country's population healthier. However, these resources are needed by the poorer countries. Additionally, these fisheries are not limitless, so if they are overharvested and marine ecosystems are stressed, we could lose these sources of omega-3.” There are also environmental factors, including ocean warming, increased ocean acidity, overfishing, and other pollutants are depleting natural omega-3 resources. Often, what is left is contaminated with polychlorinated biphenyl, methylmercury, phthalates, flame retardants, perfluorinated chemicals, pesticides, and plastics. Some oils, including chia seed, flax seed, canola, olive, fish, and plants, particularly Purslane (which is often considered an unwanted weed) are some cheaper sources of omega-3s. Omega-3 supplements can also be purchased at the neighborhood pharmacy or grocery store. However, these supplements are often not standardized in dose or tested for lipid oxidation or contaminants. Thus, it is possible that non-oxidized omega-3 that comes from fresh food may be a better way to get these fats. However, this remains an area for future research. If we don't act fast by protecting oceans, stewarding fisheries, and engaging in sustainable and regenerative aquaculture, this already inaccessible source may be even less available for future generations, Ciesielski stressed. “What we have here is a deficiency that can cause many serious problems—not just in a subset of humanity, [but] in most of us,” Ciesielski said. “So, the scale of this is pretty striking. Now, is the research is perfect? No, but the intention of this article is to start the conversation.” Ciesielski calls for a more equitable food industry—one that prioritizes environmental and human health to ensure all people, regardless of location or socioeconomic status, have access to foods that support healthy living and help prevent diseases like cancer. Elizabeth Galamba is a contributing writer.
