AB  - Aquatic foods have formed an important part of our diets for centuries. Some of the first evidence of fishing comes from eastern Africa, based on bone harpoons found on the lakeshore of Ishango in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, dating back to 90,000 years ago (Braun et al., 2010). In the UK, herring dominated fish consumption in the Middle Ages, initially becoming a favorite food of the high-class society in the 13th and 14th centuries but then becoming a regular food for the common person 100 years later (Caux, 1881). In the 17th century, eating fish as a substitute for other animal products was authorized, which helped to create a stronger demand for fish, especially salmon, and in the 1780s, Perth's merchants shipped the first salmon packed in ice to London's Billingsgate fish market (Coates, 2006). The popularity of salmon was increasing dramatically, and by the mid-1870s it had become a signature food for the British Empire, with cases being shipped to Calcutta (now Kolkata), Cape Town, Melbourne, and Liverpool. Around that time, Scotland also had developed one of the largest herring fisheries in the world (Caux, 1881). It was at that time when Francis (Frank) Buckland, in his capacity as naturalist, conservationist, and “fish culturalist to the Queen,” called for research into fisheries, as fish stocks began to decline. But at the same time he also advocated the importance of fish in the diet, especially for those on lower incomes. His views on science underpinning the management of fish stocks in relation to trade and consumption are as actual and critical now as they were then. Fish is one of only two food groups for which we currently have food-based dietary guidelines in the UK (with the other one being fruits and vegetables), highlighting the importance of fish consumption for human health. The guidelines for fish consumption are based on evidence from multiple studies indicating that either low (one serving per week) or moderate fish consumption (two to four servings per week) reduces the risk of mortality from coronary heart disease by 16% and 21%, respectively (Zheng et al., 2012). The health benefits of eating fish have traditionally been attributed to its levels of marine-derived omega-3 fatty acids, and recently it was shown that those with the highest levels of blood omega-3 fatty acids have up to an 18% lower risk of dying from heart disease, cancer, or indeed other causes, compared with those with the lowest levels of blood omega-3 fatty acids (Harris et al., 2021). The evidence for the health benefits of fish is much stronger than that for fish oil, and omega-3 supplements are probably not very useful in preventing or treating cardiovascular disease: 167 people will need to take omega-3 supplements for c. 4 years so that one of those people avoids a coronary heart disease event (Abdelhamid et al., 2020). In addition to omega-3 fatty acids, fish is our most important dietary source of vitamin D, which is important for bone health but increasingly is also believed to beneficially impact on a range of other important chronic conditions such as diabetes, heart disease, various cancers, and multiple sclerosis. Although the most important source of vitamin D in humans is ultraviolet B–induced dermal synthesis of cholecalciferol, food sources, like oily fish, can play an important role in maintaining vitamin D status during winter, when low ultraviolet B availability prompts a high prevalence of vitamin D deficiency in many northern European countries (O'Neill et al., 2016). Furthermore, fish is an important source of a range of micronutrients, including vitamins A, B12, and D, as well as iodine, iron, selenium, zinc, and calcium (de Roos et al., 2017), and some have argued that a decrease in fish catch, due to declining numbers of marine fish, could make 10% of the global population vulnerable to micronutrient and fatty acid deficiencies, especially those in developing nations where fish plays an important role in protein and energy intake (Golden et al., 2016). This highlights the urgent need for nutrition-sensitive fisheries policies and of aquatic food production systems, but at the same time also of fish-sensitive dietary guidelines (de Roos, 2023), to optimize the benefits of fish consumption under challenging conditions, including climate change, degradation of marine habitats, environmental pollution, and declining biodiversity, causing decreasing fish stocks and yields (Figure 1). Global supplies of fish are dependent on wild stocks but increasingly also on aquaculture production systems (FAO, 2022). Fisheries and aquatic farms play key roles in providing food and nutrient security, but production policies are mostly aimed at maximizing economic performance, with insufficient consideration for the nutritional quality of the fish being produced (de Roos, 2023). However, novel fisheries management strategies have been proposed to optimize fisheries output for nutrients. For example, analysis of global catch data suggests that applying the concept of multispecies maximum nutrient yield can potentially have an impact on nutrient delivery in regions where fish is a critical source of dietary micronutrients (Robinson et al., 2022). Furthermore, in our group we combined top-down population modeling approaches with bottom-up nutrient compositional analysis to develop fishing at maximum sustainable nutritional yield, the rate at which essential nutrients are maximized (unpublished data). The Blue Food Assessment, a new geographical framework to assess the relevance of policy objectives in relation to aquatic food production and consumption, highlighted that consumption of aquatic foods could ameliorate vitamin B12 and omega-3 deficiencies in many African and South American countries, whereas moderate consumption of seafood, replacing the consumption of ruminant meat, could lower the prevalence of cardiovascular diseases while lowering greenhouse gas footprints in many northern nations (Crona et al., 2023). But for most countries we need more context-specific data linking aquatic food production and supply chains with dietary recommendations, consumption levels, and nutritional status to fully understand the benefits and trade-offs of such policy objectives. On the contrary, dietary recommendations for fish consumption are not necessarily considering whether national fish supplies satisfy recommendations. In fact, less than half of the European dietary recommendations for fish consumption are satisfied by national seafood supplies. Most of the countries with coastal access, or those with traditional fish-eating cultures, have adequate seafood supplies, whereas landlocked countries, as well as the UK, do not have enough seafood supplies to satisfy dietary recommendations (Lofstedt et al., 2021). This begs the question whether current national dietary recommendations, which already vary significantly between countries, need to be revisited to allow for a more national and targeted approach to sustainable nutrient provision. The UK dietary recommendation to eat two fish a week, of which one should be oily, has not been effective in increasing oily fish consumption—on average we are eating less than half of what we should be eating (e.g., 110 rather than 280 g per person per week) for optimal health benefits, and this level of consumption has hardly changed in the past two decades (NDNS, 2020). Mapping national seafood supplies, and assessing how UK production and supply chains could be better aligned with consumption and dietary recommendations, will be necessary to optimize nutritional impact while at the same time considering increasingly important issues in relation to planetary health. Fish will arguably become a more important component of our diets as we transform toward more environmentally sustainable food systems to achieve net zero by 2050. Most seafoods have lower greenhouse gas emissions while providing more nutrients than terrestrial alternatives—with nutrient density scores being c. 1.5–2 times higher in salmonids and pelagics compared to chicken, and the amount of kg CO2 equivalents per kilogram edible product in salmonids, pelagics, and bivalves being only a fraction of that in beef and pork (Bianchi et al., 2022). Indeed, modeling studies aiming to optimize nutritional intake and human health while minimizing greenhouse gas emissions found that fish was the most common item to be added to habitual UK diets (Horgan et al., 2016). However, simply increasing the demand for the fish we are used to eating may not be a sustainable solution, and instead we may want to consider shifting consumer demand from the “big five” (cod, haddock, tuna, salmon, and prawns) to preferences that are better aligned with local and national production. This would address a growing mismatch between seafood production and supply versus consumption in the UK, where the majority of fish, and its valuable nutrients, is exported and the majority of fish we consume is imported (Harrison et al., 2023). But efforts to increase fish consumption will depend on consumer demand, with factors such as taste, price, and availability of attractive options that are easy to prepare being especially important from a consumer perspective (Carlucci et al., 2015), as well as on supplies, which are significantly influenced by trade policies (Willer & Aldridge, 2023). I am sure Frank Buckland would have been delighted to see how his work on the natural history of British fishes and fisheries science (Buckland, 1881), and the subsequent advances in fisheries and aquaculture sciences, but also in nutritional sciences, in the past centenary, is now addressing our food security challenges of the 21st century. Food, including seafood, is the single strongest lever to optimize human health, environmental sustainability, and biodiversity on Earth, but we need context-specific and cross-disciplinary data and insights on how we can sustainably manage our fish stocks and production in relation to what we trade and consume, to develop better-informed fisheries and nutrition policies. The research of Baukje de Roos is supported by the Scottish Government Rural and Environment Science and Analytical Services Division (RESAS; RI-B5-04).
