AB  - Stormwater runoff is a major non-point source of effluent that is commonly discharged to the urban watershed. As runoff moves across different landscapes, it accumulates nutrients and a complex mixture of chemicals that can adversely affect receiving aquatic systems. Nose Creek (NC) is a stormwater dominated watershed receiving discharge from more than 137 outfalls. NC flows into the Bow River (BR) upstream of the Calgary’s municipal wastewater effluent (MWWE) discharge. This study provides updated evidence of biological responses in fish exposed to the stormwater within the Nose Creek Watershed and in the Bow River upstream and downstream of the MWWE. Whole-organism endpoints in longnose dace (Rhinichthys cataractae) were significantly elevated in NC compared with the BR. Transcriptomic analysis showed that longnose dace downstream of the NC–BR confluence exhibited alterations in the same biochemical pathways observed in NC fish. The high degree of overlap in dysregulated genes between NC and downstream BR site suggests that NC inputs may contribute to the downstream response. Fathead minnow (Pimephales promelas) populations within the Nose Creek Watershed exhibited alteration of biochemical pathways related to xenobiotic metabolism, cellular stress response, and immune function suggesting that stormwater runoff may be contributing to immune-adaptive and cellular stress responses. Caging studies demonstrated that rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) exposed in experimental raceways receiving 5% MWWE displayed disrupted metabolic and endocrine pathways. Caged rainbow trout exposed to stormwater in West Nose Creek also showed pronounced transcriptomic responses, including dysregulation of energy metabolism, cellular stress response, and xenobiotic metabolism. These results indicate that stormwater inputs can pose eco-physiological risks comparable to MWWE, with acute biological stress amplified during rainfall events.
