AB  - <jats:p>CRISPR-Cas9 has become one of the most widely used genome-editing technologies for achieving precise, locus-specific modification in diverse organisms, and in this study it was applied in combination with homology-directed repair (HDR) to introduce the Salmo salar elongation of very long-chain fatty acids–like 2 (Elovl2) gene into the zebrafish (Danio rerio) genome during early embryogenesis. To enable targeted integration, a donor plasmid containing the full Salmo salar Elovl2 coding sequence flanked by 1-kb homology arms was engineered to align with the first start codon of the endogenous zebrafish Elovl2 locus. Microinjections were performed on wild-type embryos at the one-cell stage, approximately 10–15 minutes post-fertilization, using a 1.5-nl mixture of sgRNA, Cas9 protein, donor plasmid, phenol red, and nuclease-free water. Integration efficiency and specificity were assessed through PCR amplification of junction fragments, Sanger sequencing, and fluorescence microscopy using enhanced green fluorescent protein (EGFP) as a reporter. Roughly 67% of injected embryos displayed statistically significant EGFP expression (P &lt; 0.001) in the head, trunk, and tail, indicating robust HDR-mediated knock-in activity. Sanger sequencing confirmed the precise insertion of the Salmo salar Elovl2 sequence by identifying both homology arm regions and the intact coding region at the targeted start codon, and in vitro assays further demonstrated accurate sgRNA-directed Cas9 cleavage. RT-qPCR revealed that transgenic embryos expressed approximately seven-fold higher levels of Elovl2 relative to wild-type controls (P &lt; 0.01), confirming successful transcription of the integrated gene. However, the transgenic group exhibited elevated mortality during the 5–24 hpf hatching period, suggesting potential developmental sensitivity associated with genome editing or transgene expression. Overall, these results establish an efficient CRISPR-Cas9 HDR approach for generating Elovl2 knock-in zebrafish and highlight its potential for enhancing omega-3 fatty acid biosynthesis traits in aquaculture species.</jats:p>
