AB  - <jats:p>The presence and distribution of 17β-estradiol (E2) in muscle tissue from farmed salmonids in Chile was assessed to provide an evidence-based baseline and to establish physiological reference ranges of endogenous 17β-estradiol (E2) in farmed salmonids from Chile. To this aim, four hundred samples from Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar), coho salmon (Oncorhynchus kisutch), and rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) were analyzed. These samples were collected through a stratified sampling design with longitudinal follow-up at aquaculture farms. E2 concentrations were determined using GC-MS/MS and UHPLC-MS/MS in muscle and plasma matrices. The analyte was detected in 28.25% of the muscle samples (≤ 0.85 µg/kg), with higher detection frequency in females and heavier fish, particularly in coho salmon (Coho salmon: 104; Rainbow trout: 4; Atlantic salmon: 5). The generalized linear model identified species, sex, and gonadosomatic index as the main predictors of E2 presence, with high discriminatory power (AUC = 0.90). Due to the limited detected sample size for Rainbow trout and Atlantic salmon, reference physiological ranges were exclusively established for Coho salmon muscle (≤0.15 μg/kg for T2 [&gt;1 kg to 2 kg]  and ≤0.41 μg/kg for T3 [2 kg]). Estimated human exposure to E2 from salmon consumption was several orders of magnitude lower than the acceptable daily intake defined by international authorities, with risk ratios below 1 in all scenarios. These findings confirm that E2 is naturally present in farmed salmonids at concentrations consistent with physiological processes and provide robust baseline data to support risk-based assessments and the differentiation between endogenous and atypical hormone levels in aquaculture products.</jats:p>
