AB  - <jats:p>Aquaculture-derived organic matter (AOM) substantially alters seafloor ecosystems through organic enrichment, yet the full three-dimensional extent of these effects in cold-water high-latitude environments remains poorly quantified. We present an integrated investigation combining horizontal distribution patterns and vertical sediment profiles of AOM effects around Atlantic salmon farms in Berufjörður, eastern Iceland. Horizontal transects were established around a farm (≈4000 t biomass) at distances of 0–1000 m from cage edges; sediment cores from active, fallowed, edge and reference sites were sectioned by depth (0–2, 2–5, 5–10 and 10–20 cm). Parameters included sediment organic carbon content, carbon reactivity (O₂ consumption normalised to Corg), stable isotopes (δ13C, δ15N) and macrofaunal community analysis. Results revealed a pronounced exponential decline in horizontal impact with distance from farm cages (r² = 0.83–0.95). Carbon reactivity emerged as the most sensitive impact indicator, exceeding reference values by 33–81-fold at cage sites (1000–1153 µmol O₂ g-1 C h-1) versus reference sites (30–50 µmol O₂ g-1 C h-1). Vertically, active farming sites exhibited extreme surface reactivity declining exponentially with depth but remaining significantly elevated at 10–20 cm, indicative of subsurface organic matter persistence. Capitella capitata dominated cage-adjacent assemblages (up to 92.6% of individuals) and penetrated to 10 cm sediment depth beneath active farms — deeper than previously documented for aquaculture-impacted sediments. Fallowed sites showed partial surface recovery but persistent organic enrichment at depth, indicating that surface-only monitoring overestimates recovery. Horizontal impacts were constrained to &lt;150 m except along residual current directions, whereas vertical impacts extended into deeper sediment layers; together these findings necessitate three-dimensional monitoring protocols for sustainable aquaculture management in high-latitude fjord systems.</jats:p>
