AB  - The replacement of marine feed ingredients with plant-based alternatives in salmon feed lowers eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA) content in farmed Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar). This study quantifies the relative contributions of host genetics and gut microbiome composition to phenotypic variation in omega-3 and other fatty acid traits in Atlantic salmon. The gut microbiome was characterized using 16S rRNA gene sequencing of 425 Atlantic salmon in the seawater phase, genotyped with a 70K AquaGen SNP array. Host genome explained a larger proportion of phenotypic variation than microbial effects across fatty acid traits. Gut microbiome effects were statistically significant for EPA (m² = 17%) and DHA (m² = 12%). Individual microbial taxa showed low to moderate heritability (up to h² = 0.25), indicating host genetic influence on microbiome composition. Microbiome-trait association analyses identified Caldanaerobius as positively associated and Lactobacillus as negatively associated with EPA, and Romboutsia as positively associated with α-linolenic acid (ALA). Both host genetics and gut microbiome composition shape omega-3 fatty acid traits in Atlantic salmon, with host genetics accounting for a greater proportion of phenotypic variance. These indicate potential for incorporating gut microbiome composition alongside genomic selection to improve omega-3 level in farmed salmon.
