AB  - <jats:p>The fish gut microbiota is often referred to as an “extra organ” due to its pivotal role in host metabolism, nutrition, and immunity. Yet, for most fish species, the precise complementary functional potentials between the fish gut microbiome and its host remain poorly defined. Here, we used shotgun metagenomic sequencing and KEGG module analysis to compare the functional potential of the gut microbiome with that of the host genome in captive juvenile chum salmon (Oncorhynchus keta) at 35 days post-hatching, a critical period immediately after the onset of exogenous feeding. Using the GenompleTM platform and the publicly available chum salmon genome, we calculated Module Completion Ratios (MCRs) for metabolic pathways in both the host and gut metagenome. Because the small guts of juveniles yield low microbial biomass and a high proportion of host DNA, we first optimized a DNA extraction and preparation protocol based on enzymatic lysis and NEBNext®-based host DNA depletion. This approach markedly improved microbial DNA yield and functional annotation depth in the metagenomes. Comparative “functionome” analysis then revealed a striking pattern of functional complementation: the chum salmon genome was incomplete for the de novo biosynthesis of several essential amino acids (threonine, leucine, valine/isoleucine, and lysine) and B-vitamins (thiamine, riboflavin, and biotin), whereas the gut microbiome encoded complete or nearly complete modules for these pathways. Taxa affiliated with Bartonella, Acinetobacter, and Enterococcus were identified as major contributors to these functions. Our results provide direct metagenomic evidence that the juvenile chum salmon gut microbiota potentially complement critical biosynthetic capacity for essential nutrients during early development, with implications for aquaculture feed formulation and the nutritional resilience of wild salmon populations facing environmental change like ocean warming and habitat changes.</jats:p>
