AB  - <jats:p>Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) is a promising cold‑water species for seawater aquaculture. Inappropriate salinity acclimation may induce oxidative stress, tissue damage, and metabolic imbalance. In this study, using large‑sized rainbow trout, we evaluated the optimal seawater acclimation strategy from the perspectives of oxidative stress, digestion and absorption, and intestinal metabolomic remodeling. Four different salinity increase rates (4, 6, 8, and 16 ppt/day) were applied to gradually acclimate the fish from 14 ppt to 30 ppt, with freshwater‑reared fish serving as the control. After reaching the target salinity, we assessed antioxidant enzyme activities, histopathological changes, expression of growth‑ and absorption‑related genes, digestive enzyme activities, and untargeted intestinal metabolomics. The results showed that rapid salinity increase exacerbated gill and liver damage, accompanied by elevated levels of SOD, CAT, GSH, and MDA, particularly in the T16 group. In contrast, the T4 group exhibited only mild and reversible gill edema without obvious liver damage, and showed moderate activation of the antioxidant defense system, indicating a lower oxidative cost. Meanwhile, T4 significantly upregulated the expression of igf1 and sik1, enhanced AKP activity in the intestine, gills, and liver, and maintained relatively stable lipid digestion. Metabolomic analysis identified a total of 1,644 intestinal metabolites. KEGG enrichment analysis of differential metabolites revealed that the ABC transporter and biosynthesis of amino acids pathways were the two most important pathways during salinity adaptation. Furthermore, multiple amino acids were significantly increased, suggesting enhanced amino acid transport and metabolic compensation during seawater acclimation. Overall, a slow salinity increase at a rate of 4 ppt/day promotes the adaptation of large‑sized rainbow trout to seawater environments by alleviating oxidative damage and remodeling intestinal amino acid metabolism, providing a practical strategy for seawater aquaculture of rainbow trout.</jats:p>
