AB  - <title>Abstract</title>
                <p>Background
 Gill damage compromises gas exchange, ion regulation, and disease resistance in farmed Atlantic salmon, yet the molecular basis of gill filament regeneration remains underexplored. This study characterized transcriptomic changes during gill filament regeneration at seven time points over 20 weeks post-resection and integrated these data with functional recovery metrics.
Results
 RNA-seq revealed pronounced early transcriptomic disruption that progressively resolved as regeneration advanced. Clustering and Gene Ontology enrichment analyses identified a phased molecular program. The early phase (Weeks 1–4) featured transient induction of genes associated with inflammation, blastema formation, and initial tissue patterning. The mid phase (Weeks 4–12) showed enrichment of morphogenetic processes, including contraction and cartilage formation. The late phase (Weeks 12–20) exhibited gene expression signatures indicative of cartilage and neural consolidation. These transitions coincided with the recovery of transcripts related to ionoregulation. Correlation analyses indicated that prolonged signatures of multipotency and proliferation were negatively associated with regeneration outcomes, whereas markers of differentiation and tissue patterning showed positive associations.
Conclusions
 This study defines a transcriptional timeline of gill filament regeneration in Atlantic salmon, providing a foundation for future mechanistic investigations of the molecular drivers of repair and the development of strategies to preserve gill integrity and function in aquaculture.</p>
