AB  - This thesis explores Religious Studies and Geographic approaches to a new ecological crisis arising from human-salmon relationships in aquaculture facilities. Salmon farming is threatened by a series of industry-limiting diseases which threatens livelihoods, changes coastal ecologies, and determines the life experiences of hundreds of millions of animals. Super high densities and porous farm structures mean that salmon farming both creates, and generates susceptibility to, new diseases. With a poor environmental record and a rapidly decreasing social license to operate, members of human-salmon societies desperately need new narratives of living-with each other. The thesis explores the many factors which build salmon-worlds, including the narratives, value-systems and physical ephemera, which make human-salmon relationships possible. The goal here is to achieve the fullest possible accounting of actors in the network, to uncover factors with underappreciated ordering power, particularly ecological understandings held in social narratives and oral cultures, which condition the material arrangements of farming practices. By staging an encounter between salmon and their worlds, and human world-making narratives, this thesis explores how salmon accumulate cultural significance and agentic capacity over time through historical processes of engagement. The result is an exploration of how salmon, in the Atlantic, Tasman, and Pacific Northwestern contexts, are co-authors of ‘narrative’ ecologies. An understanding of the salmons’ making-of these cultures could contribute to better outcomes than current paradigms of agricultural control. The thesis makes three principal original contributions: It reveals how religious frameworks, particularly the ontological positioning of nonhumans, have shaped practices and legislative frameworks governing salmon. Second, it demonstrates how narrative investigation provides access to dimensions of material practice that are otherwise obscured. Finally, it argues that these narrative ecologies constitute an essential component of the totality of human-nonhuman relationships, conditioning material practices and ecological relations in ways that must be addressed for more sustainable futures to emerge.
