AB  - <jats:p>This research evaluated the effects of dietary arginine (Arg) supplementation on the growth, physiological metabolism, intestinal health and disease resistance of triploid rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) fed high-carbohydrate diets (HCD). Five experimental diets were designed, including a normal carbohydrate control diet (NC) and four HCDs with 0% (HC), 0.5% (HC+0.5%Arg), 1% (HC+1%Arg), and 1.5% (HC+1.5%Arg) Arg supplementation, respectively. A total of 450 rainbow trout (initial body weight: 10.20±0.20 g) were randomly assigned to 15 tanks (3 replicates, each replicate containing 30 fish) and fed for 8 weeks. The results indicated that, in comparison with the HC group, HC+1%Arg significantly raised final body weight (FBW), weight gain rate (WGR), and specific growth rate (SGR), while reducing feed conversion ratio (FCR) (P&lt;0.05). Quadratic regression analysis indicated WGR was optimized at an Arg concentration of 2.94% in HCD. HC induced significantly higher GLU level than NC, but Arg supplementation reduced GLU level (P&lt;0.05). Intestinal amylase activity was highest in HC+0.5%Arg, and HC+1%Arg had higher total superoxide dismutase (T-SOD) and lower malonaldehyde (MDA) than HC (P&lt;0.05). 0.5%-1% Arg supplementation alleviated HC-induced intestinal damage, increasing villus height and microvillus length (P&lt;0.05). Arg also upregulated intestinal tight junction genes (claudin7, ZO1, occludin) and immune-related genes (TGF-β), while downregulated pro-inflammatory (MyD88, IL-1β, TNF-α). In the liver, Arg supplementation upregulated the expression of GLUT1, GK, HK, and PPARα genes (P&lt;0.05). For intestinal microbiota, HC+1%Arg significantly increased α-diversity and abundance of beneficial bacteria (Lactococcus) (P&lt;0.05). Challenge test with Aeromonas hydrophila showed that Arg-supplemented groups had higher survival rates, with HC+1%Arg group reaching 50.0% survival rate on day 5 post-injection, significantly higher than all other groups (P&lt;0.05). In conclusion, 1% Arg supplementation in HCD effectively mitigates high-carbohydrate stress in triploid rainbow trout by improving growth, promoting glucolipid metabolism, enhancing intestinal health, and optimizing microbiota. This study provides a theoretical foundation for the application of Arg in HCD for triploid rainbow trout and contributes to the sustainable development of cold-water fish aquaculture.</jats:p>
