AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>
                    This article engages with justification theory to explore the underlying assumptions, logics, and methodological approaches that shape common good arguments concerning the value and use of marine nature. Seven distinct “orders of worth”—
                    <jats:italic>Market</jats:italic>
                    ,
                    <jats:italic>Industrial</jats:italic>
                    ,
                    <jats:italic>Civic</jats:italic>
                    ,
                    <jats:italic>Fame</jats:italic>
                    ,
                    <jats:italic>Domestic, Inspired, and Green—</jats:italic>
                    are each explored in the context of the marine. Each order offers distinct normative claims on what is right or wrong in how we use and value marine nature and spaces, using particular language resources, worthy objects and subjects to argue these principled positions. Examples are drawn from seaweed harvesting and salmon farming, particularly as these relate to two dispute cases in Ireland, to illustrate the distinct arguments and justification processes relating to marine value. This article contributes to understandings of the values and politics shaping the future of marine space and nature.
                  </jats:p>
