AB  - Salmonid production in Chile is based mainly on Atlantic salmon, coho salmon, and rainbow trout, three species introduced into the country at the beginning of the twentieth century to develop sport fishing. It was only some 70–80 years later that industrial development began. In rainbow trout, the most frequently used reproductive biotechnology is to produce all-female populations. Sperm cryopreservation is applied in all three species, with the widest application and the best results found in Atlantic salmon. In Chile, at laboratory scale, at least the most used cryopreservation protocol is composed of 1.3 M dimethylsulfoxide, 0.3 M glucose, and 2% bovine serum albumin (BSA) diluted in Cortland solution has been used for Atlantic salmon, while early studies in coho salmon indicate that 8-10% dimethylsulfoxide supplemented with 0.18 M glucose and 10% hen egg yolk could be useful. Straws of 0.25 and 0.5 mL are regularly used for experimental purposes; on a large scale, Chilean companies use 0.5 ml straws and squarepacks. On the other hand, the freezing method generally reported across the industry is the uncontrolled system, which usually results in variable responses regarding post-thaw motility and fertility.
