AB  - <jats:p>Salmon lice pose a major threat to both farmed and wild salmon, with rising resistance to chemical treatments highlighting the need for integrated control strategies and a deeper understanding of their life cycle and dispersal. To assess lice loads and potential impacts on wild fish, hydrodynamic particle tracking models have been developed globally, though their accuracy varies due to differences in structure, input data, and assumptions—particularly when modelling larval stages. In Scotland, multiple biophysical models are regularly used, each demonstrating strengths and weaknesses depending on application. Validation against field data, including plankton tows and sentinel cage results, revealed inconsistencies in individual model performance. However, combining models into a multi-model ensemble yielded more accurate and stable predictions, offering a clearer and more balanced understanding of lice distributions. This ensemble approach enhances risk management in aquaculture and supports informed decision-making, especially when paired with transparent communication of model uncertainty—both from data limitations (aleatoric) and model design choices (epistemic).</jats:p>
