AB  - While synthetic antibiotics have long served as growth promoters in aquaculture, mounting concerns over antimicrobial resistance and consumer safety have accelerated the search for natural alternatives. Oregano essential oil (OEO), rich in the phenolic compounds carvacrol and thymol, has demonstrated antimicrobial, antioxidant, and immunostimulatory properties in several terrestrial livestock species, yet its effects on salmonid fish remain incompletely characterised. This research evaluated the impact of dietary OEO supplementation at 0, 100, 200, 400, and 800 mg/kg feed on growth performance, intestinal morphology, and immune-related gene expression in juvenile rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) over a 56-day feeding trial. A total of 300 fish (initial weight 28.4±2.1 g) were randomly distributed into 30 tanks (10 fish per tank, 6 replicates per treatment) at the Trabzon Institute of Fisheries Sciences. Fish fed 400 mg/kg OEO achieved the highest specific growth rate (1.83±0.06%/day vs. 1.42±0.05 in controls; p&lt;0.001) and the lowest feed conversion ratio (1.24±0.04 vs. 1.51±0.06; p&lt;0.001). Intestinal villus height increased by 28.7% at 400 mg/kg, while crypt depth decreased by 19.7%, yielding a 59.8% improvement in villus-to-crypt ratio. Quantitative RT-PCR analysis of anterior intestine and head kidney tissues showed significant upregulation of interleukin-1? (3.4-fold), tumour necrosis factor-? (2.8-fold), immunoglobulin M (2.2-fold), and lysozyme (1.9-fold) at 400 mg/kg. No additional growth or immune benefit was observed at 800 mg/kg, and mild hepatocyte vacillation was noted histologically in that group. These findings identify 400 mg/kg as the optimal dietary OEO inclusion level for rainbow trout, providing concurrent growth promotion, gut structural enhancement, and innate immune stimulation without adverse effects.
