AB  - <jats:p>Tasmanian Atlantic salmon production occasionally experiences suboptimum growth conditions driven by climate change. Using a recirculating aquaculture system, we compared the cumulative effects of suboptimum temperature and low dissolved oxygen (DO) (19 °C and 80% DO) over 74 days (post-thermal/PT; summer stress), followed by an autumn recovery period of 42 days at 15 °C and 100% DO (post-recovery/PR), on the nutritional physiology of post-smolt Atlantic salmon, using whole-animal bioenergetics combined with liver and white muscle proteomics. Our comparison included performance groups predicted to show high, medium, and low growth performance based on the historical data. Predicted-performance groups showed significant differences (high &gt; medium &gt; low) in recovery growth, which prompted comparative proteomic analysis that revealed enrichment of immunomodulation and cellular degradation proteins in the low-performance group compared to the high-performance group. Whole-body, tissue chemical composition and nutrient retention efficiency were significantly different between PT and PR groups. In the liver, functional enrichment analysis of the ~2,000 proteins elevated at PT identified proteins related to antioxidant defence and endoplasmic reticulum stress, including the HSP70 and HSP90 families and HSP47/SERPINh1, while unsaturated fatty acid biosynthesis and translation were significant for n = 1569 liver proteins abundant in the PR group. Liver fatty acid composition indicated increased 18:0 and 20:4n-6 and decreased 18:1n-9 and 18:2n-6 at PR. Protein turnover was the most affected biological process in the white muscle. Overall, the findings indicate protein turnover as the key mechanistic driver of growth performance and growth efficiency in Atlantic salmon under changing climate conditions.</jats:p>
