AB  - Many biological (e.g., age, weight, condition, run timing, migratory route) and environmental factors (e.g., water temperature, salinity, wind and water currents) influencing wild Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) smolt, can contribute to the increased probability of sea lice (e.g., Caligus spp. and Lepeophtheirus spp.) exposure and susceptibility in the marine environment. Here, six moored sentinel cages stocked with wild Atlantic salmon smolt (Chapter 2), and a randomized control trial (RCT), consisting of paired releases of 4,024 antiparasitic-treated (emamectin benzoate; EMB) and 4,023 sham-treated wild Atlantic salmon smolt (Chapter 3) were used to address the question of what effect sea lice infestation might have on wild Atlantic salmon smolt survival during migration through coastal marine environments with and without salmonid aquaculture? Average survival for wild Conne River smolt stocked in sentinel cages in Bay d'Espoir (area with aquaculture) was 64.1% but varied from 35.5% to 84.8%. Average survival for wild Campbellton River smolt stocked in sentinel cages in Notre Dame Bay (area without aquaculture) was higher at 73.5%, less variable, and ranged from 69.7% to 78.1%. Differences in transport time prior to stocking, environmental variables (i.e., water temperature, salinity, dissolved oxygen, depth), and loss of body condition among wild Atlantic salmon smolt housed in sentinel cages, showed no significant correlation with survival. Health assessments revealed no external parasites, including sea lice. Kidney and heart samples of wild smolt from both locations were negative for infectious salmon anemia virus. Prior to stocking, histological analysis revealed no epicarditis in the Conne River smolt (area with aquaculture) and mild epicarditis in 6.7% of the Campbellton River smolt (area without aquaculture). Following deployment, histological analysis revealed mild epicarditis in 5.7% of the Conne River smolt, and 7.7% of the Campbellton River smolt. For the RCT, the effect of EMB treatment on adult returns was dependent on the river system, with more sham-treated smolt compared to EMB-treated smolt returning as adults to Conne River (area with aquaculture) and conversely, more EMB-treated smolt compared to sham-treated smolt returning as adults to Campbellton River (area without aquaculture). While the pattern was consistent between years for the Campbellton River, this was not the case for Conne River. Together, results of my thesis affirm that sea lice can likely be a source of direct or indirect marine mortality for wild Atlantic salmon smolt even in areas without salmonid aquaculture, and the importance of establishing non-aquaculture reference baselines and accounting for interannual variability when interpreting farmed and wild interactions, best studied using complementary multi-design approaches.
