AB  - <jats:p>Hydrogen sulfide ($H_{2}S$) is a critical risk factor in recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS), yet its short-term dynamics are poorly resolved at conventional measurement sensitivities. We applied continuous sub-$\mu$g/L electrochemical monitoring with a compartment-resolved total-sulfide ($S_{T}$) mass balance to a commercial 4700 $m^{3}$ Atlantic salmon RAS ($\sim$112 t biomass) over several weeks. Baseline $H_{2}S$ remained well below conventional detection limits ($&lt;0.1$ $\mu$g/L) but showed structured, reproducible variability on hourly to daily timescales. The response to feeding was regime-dependent: in this configuration, below $\sim$1000 kg day$^{-1}$ ($\sim$1\% biomass/day) fluctuations were weak and decoupled, while above this threshold an impulse-like $H_{2}S$ excursion followed each feeding event. Phase-space analysis indicates that this response is driven by feeding-induced $CO_{2}$ pulses that depress pH and shift speciation of an existing $S_{T}$ reservoir toward volatile $H_{2}S$ -- a ``chemical unlock&amp;apos;&amp;apos; mechanism [Meriac et al. 2026]. Notably, we report a feed-independent excursion during confirmed respiratory distress, in which the inferred sulfide source shifted from the biofilter to the fish tank at $\sim$1 $\mu$g h$^{-1}$ kg$^{-1}$ biomass. The signal is consistent with an endogenous, stress-associated biological contribution, plausibly mediated by gasotransmitter pathways and amplified across the synchronized 100-t biomass -- a hypothesis-generating observation motivating targeted physiological work. These results indicate that $H_{2}S$ risk in RAS is state-dependent, jointly governed by sulfide inventory, speciation, and metabolic forcing, and that sub-$\mu$g/L resolution is a prerequisite for resolving its dynamics.</jats:p>
