AB  - Salmon lice, ectoparasites of salmonids, pose a significant challenge to the aquaculture of Atlantic salmon, limiting industry growth, increasing the production costs, and compromising fish welfare. Various methods have been developed to control the parasites, including the treatment of the host fish with freshwater. This review summarizes current knowledge on the tolerance of salmon lice to lower salinities and freshwater derived from laboratory experiments. It contrasts these findings with field studies conducted under typical production conditions, revealing notable discrepancies between lab and field settings. These differences illustrate the additional impact of handling during freshwater treatments in the field. Furthermore, the literature on the use of ion-modified water is reviewed, which has shown promising results. The potential for increased tolerance or resistance to freshwater, knowledge gaps, and implications for using freshwater as a delousing agent are discussed with recommendations for further research.
