AB  - This is version 2 of the manuscript, prepared for submission to Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews. The article has been substantively revised, expanded from approximately 8,500 to 10,900 words, and retitled to lead with sustainability and waste-heat recovery rather than materials design alone. Operational deployment coverage has grown from six to seventeen sites across nine countries on three continents, including the world’s largest project (Microsoft and Fortum at Espoo and Kirkkonummi, Finland) and two Chinese sites identified through non-English literature search. A direct-to-chip cold-plate cooling section has been added as a parallel first-generation architecture, addressing the dominant production cooling design among hyperscale AI operators in 2025. The methodology now follows PRISMA 2020 reporting standards with a flow diagram in supplementary material. The lifecycle CO2 model has been extended with a sensitivity analysis across utilisation rate and displaced-fuel mix, and embodied-emissions accounting for the cooling fluid itself has been added. Reference list expanded from 37 to 67 entries, all in Elsevier numbered format. Six figures (one removed, one new, four retained) and four tables (two new). Spelling converted to UK convention throughout the body. New deployments added: 1. Yandex Mäntsälä (Finland), 4 MW heat pump, 1,000 homes, 75 percent of town heat 2. Microsoft and Fortum (Espoo and Kirkkonummi, Finland) expanded to 350 MW combined with projected 400,000 tonnes CO2 per year 3. DigiPlex with Stockholm Exergi (Sweden), approximately 10,000 households 4. Stockholm Data Parks programme (Sweden), aggregate ~30 MW across 20-plus sites 5. Telehouse FRANKY Quarter (Frankfurt, Germany), 1,300 residential units, ~400 tCO2 per year saved (verified) 6. Cloud&Heat Eurotheum (Frankfurt, Germany), 600 kW, 60 °C, hotel and offices 7. Qarnot Computing distributed digital boilers (Bordeaux and Paris, France) 8. Deep Green (UK), swimming-pool heating in Exmouth and 19 other sites 9. Lefdal Mine + Sjømatstaden (Norway), salmon hatchery integration 10. Sigma2 supercomputer (Trondheim, Norway), salmon-farm heat coupling 11. Tencent Tianjin (China), magnetic-suspension heat pump for residential heating 12. Alibaba Cloud Hangzhou Renhe IDC (China), world’s largest immersion-cooling cluster, PUE 1.09, 70 GWh per year savings
