AB  - <jats:p>Chronic exposure to suboptimal dissolved oxygen is a common challenge in aquaculture and poses a significant risk to fish health and welfare. This study characterized performance- and welfare-related markers in Oncorhynchus mykiss subjected to long-term hypoxia. Juveniles were exposed for 30 days to 4.0 mg/L O2, while controls were maintained at 9.8 mg/L O2.The study evaluated survival rate, growth rate, condition factor, behaviour, morphometric traits, hematological and biochemical blood indices, hepatic energy stores, metabolic rate (oxygen consumption and atp5j expression); inflammation (il-6), homeostasis (cyp450a), oxidative defense (gr), and genes associated with welfare (5-ht1a, gabarap, bdnf). Sampling occurred after 15 and 30 days. No mortality occurred under hypoxia. Exposure induced both adverse and adaptive responses. Persistent negative effects included reduced growth, condition factor, and hepatosomatic index, altered hematological parameters (including increased erythrocyte surface-to-volume ratio), elevated serum aspartate aminotransferase and reduced lipase activity, indicating impaired tissue integrity. Increased il-6 expression after 15 days further reflected inflammatory stress. A sustained adaptive response was a metabolic shift toward anaerobic glycolysis, shown by elevated serum lactate and lactate dehydrogenase activity. After 30 days, additional adaptations included reduced metabolic rate, increased erythrocyte, granulocyte, and lymphocyte concentrations, elevated hemoglobin levels, and enhanced oxidative defense (upregulated glutathione reductase expression). The welfare-related genes 5-ht1a (upregulated at 15 days) and gabarap (downregulated at 30 days) suggested stress-related effects. Behaviour and major morphometric traits were unchanged. The combined analysis of these endpoints demonstrates that physiological and welfare impairment may occur well before lethal thresholds or clear behavioural abnormalities become apparent.</jats:p>
