AB  - Cod farming is a growing industry in Norway, with ambitions to expand into the global fresh fish market. However, EU regulation (EC) No. 853/2004 mandates that raw or minimally processed fishery products undergo freezing before market release to kill or devitalise viable parasites. In Norway, only farmed Atlantic salmon and rainbow trout are exempted from the freezing requirement, following two national studies documenting negligible zoonotic risk. This study investigated the presence of anisakid nematodes in Norwegian farmed cod. In total 865 cod, consisting of 748 market-size fish and 117 runts, were sourced from 11 farming localities across coastal Norway between January 2023 and September 2024. Cod fillets and viscera were inspected for anisakids using UV-press (ISO 23036-1:2021) and candling. Anisakid species identification relied on sequencing of the ITS rDNA region. In total 22 Anisakis simplex (s. s.) larvae were found, primarily in viscera, while one occurred encapsulated in the flesh. Most larvae infected market-size fish ( n = 20; P = 2.5 %), only two larvae occurred in runts ( P = 1.7 %). Fish farmed in open coastal localities showed significantly higher infection rates than cod from more sheltered fjord localities. Occasional findings of the non-zoonotic raphidascaridid nematode Hysterothylacium aduncum , along with remnants of seaweed, mussels or fish bones in non-runt cod stomachs, indicate that opportunistic feeding on free-living organisms is the main Anisakis transmission route in the cages. Overall, findings suggest that the probability of anisakids to occur in the fillets of Norwegian farmed cod is low. However, this challenges the general negligible zoonotic risk assumption of farmed fish.
