AB  - Due to climate change, summer water temperatures in Tasmania, Australia, increasingly exceed the upper thermal tolerance of Atlantic salmon, resulting in energy loss and slower growth. These unfavourable conditions affect the nutritional quality of the final product, with negative impacts on growth performance. Increasing energy storage in salmon tissue in the pre-summer period is proposed as an effective strategy to mitigate impending hot summer conditions. Given its pivotal role in lipid metabolism, dietary vitamin B5 (pantothenic acid) supplementation was hypothesised to promote higher metabolic energy storage in visceral adipose tissue and, ultimately, energy fortification. Four diets were formulated and manufactured with increasing levels of vitamin B5 (6.1, 32, 70, and 140 mg/kg diet) and fed to juvenile Atlantic salmon for 124 days. The results demonstrated that triacylglycerol concentration in visceral adipose tissue and adipocyte expansion was not affected by higher dietary vitamin B5 provision. However, feed conversion ratio decreased logarithmically ( P < 0.05) with increased levels of dietary vitamin B5. Likewise, condition factor, whole-body energy content, and dietary protein and energy retention in the whole-body of Atlantic salmon significantly increased as dietary vitamin B5 increased. These results suggest dietary vitamin B5 supplementation can improve growth performance, feed utilisation, and nutrient retention. Accordingly, Atlantic salmon fed 140 mg/kg of dietary vitamin B5 contained more whole-body energy via increasing body size compared to those fed the current commercial diets containing 70 mg/kg of dietary vitamin B5. To this end, an inclusion of dietary vitamin B5 at least 140 mg/kg is recommended to achieve fortified energy stores in whole-body towards an enhancement of summer performance of Atlantic salmon. • Triacylglycerol (TAG) storage in visceral adipose tissue (VAT) was not affected by higher dietary vitamin B5 provision. • β-oxidation activity of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) decreased logarithmically as dietary vitamin B5 inclusion increased. • Dietary vitamin B5 supplementation can improve growth performance, feed utilisation, and energy retention. • An inclusion of vitamin B5 at least 140 mg/kg is recommended for energy fortification in the whole-body of Atlantic salmon.
