AB  - <jats:p>
                    The increasing limitation of global fish oil supplies has intensified the search for sustainable lipid alternatives in aquaculture. Microalgal oils have emerged as promising sources of eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), offering the potential to reduce dependence on wild fisheries while maintaining aquafeed quality. This review evaluates the biological, nutritional, immunological, and sustainability implications of replacing fish oil with microalgal-derived omega-3 oils in salmonid aquaculture. Evidence from feeding trials involving
                    <jats:italic>Schizochytrium</jats:italic>
                    ,
                    <jats:italic>Nannochloropsis</jats:italic>
                    , and
                    <jats:italic>Crypthecodinium</jats:italic>
                    indicates that microalgal oils can partially or completely replace fish oil in selected salmonid diets without significantly compromising growth performance, feed efficiency, survival, or fillet quality under optimized formulation conditions. Several studies additionally reported feed conversion ratios below 1.0, survival rates exceeding 95%, improved DHA retention, and reduced contaminant accumulation in salmonid tissues. Beyond nutritional replacement, algal-derived omega-3 lipids influence oxidative stress regulation, mucosal barrier integrity, inflammatory signaling, gut microbiota interactions, and disease resistance, highlighting their broader functional role within the nutrient–microbiota–immunity nexus. Life-cycle assessments further indicate that microalgal oils may reduce reliance on marine feedstocks, lower environmental impacts, and support circular bioeconomy strategies. However, large-scale adoption remains constrained by high production costs, strain-specific lipid variability, oxidative instability, and energy-intensive cultivation and extraction processes. Advances in photobioreactor optimization, strain engineering, and integrated biorefinery systems are progressively improving scalability and commercial feasibility. Overall, microalgal oils represent strategically important functional lipids capable of supporting nutritionally secure, environmentally resilient, and climate-adaptive salmonid aquaculture systems.
                  </jats:p>
