AB  - Anthracnose is an important fungal disease of papaya (Carica papaya) and is caused by Colletotrichum species (Nascimento et al., 2010). Papaya is an economically important crop which is cultivated primarily in tropical and sub-tropical countries (Saini et al., 2017). In February 2022, papaya fruits displaying anthracnose symptoms were collected from Flacq, Mauritius (-20°11'22.99“ S, 57°42'51.98” E). Large, circular brown to black spots, surrounded by a pink-coloured edge, containing black fruiting bodies or salmon conidial masses were observed on affected fruit (Fig. 1). Small pieces of tissue were excised from the diseased fruit using a flame-sterilised scalpel. The pieces were surface disinfected using 1% sodium hypochlorite for three minutes followed by two rinses in sterilised distilled water. The diseased tissues were transferred onto potato dextrose agar and incubated at 25°C for seven days. Pure cultures were obtained by single hyphal tip isolation and two different types of colonies were recovered. The first fungal colony type (PapF) was greyish coloured with white aerial mycelium and light brown with brown concentric rings of growth on the underside (Fig. 2a-b). The second type (PapF2) was white and light brown to olive-coloured on the reverse side (Fig. 2c-d). The conidia (n = 30) were unicellular, straight, aseptate, with a rounded apex and acute base (Fig. 3). The mean length and width of the conidia were 11.0 ±1.1 μm × 3.0 ±0.4 μm and 12.2 ±2.2 μm × 3.4 ±0.4 μm for PapF and PapF2, respectively. 4 Fungal DNA was extracted using a CTAB-based DNA isolation method (Ranghoo & Hyde, 2000). Four genomic loci, the ITS region, GADPH, TUB2 and CHS-1 genes, were PCR-amplified and sequenced using primer pairs ITS4/ITS5 (White et al., 1990), GDF/GDR, T1/Bt2b and CHS-79F/CHS-345R (Gan et al., 2016), respectively. ITS sequences confirmed that the isolates were Colletotrichum with 100% identity to the C. gloeosporioides species complex (Accession Nos. KU534982.1 and KU534983.1). BLASTn search results showed that the GADPH and TUB2 sequences had 100% identity with KY401637.1 and JX009916.1, and MN746330.1 and CHS-1 JX009791.1, respectively, confirming that the species was C. salsolae. All sequences were deposited in Genbank under Accession Nos. OR739404-OR739405 (ITS), OR757306-OR757307 (GAPDH), OR757302-OR757303 (TUB2) and OR757304-OR757305 (CHS-1). To prove the pathogenicity of C. salsolae, four fruits of papaya cv. Solo were surface disinfected using 1% NaOCl and rinsed twice with sterile distilled water. An aliquot of a conidial suspension (50 μL, 106 conidia/mL) from each of the isolate types was separately deposited on three sites of each fruit. A control papaya fruit was inoculated with sterile distilled water and all fruits were held at room temperature. Five days after inoculation the inoculated fruits developed typical anthracnose symptoms (Figure 4) while the control fruits showed no disease symptoms. The original isolate was recovered from the inoculated fruits, thus fulfilling Koch's postulates. The experiment was repeated three times and similar observations were made on each occasion. Based on the morphological, molecular and pathogenicity analyses, the fungal isolates were identified as C. salsolae. Colletotrichum salsolae was first reported from Salsola kali subsp. tragus in Hungary and then reported on Carica papaya in India (Weir et al., 2012; Saini et al., 2017). To our knowledge, this is the first report of C. salsolae causing anthracnose of papaya in Mauritius. Dissemination of these findings will create awareness among farmers about this disease-causing agent and emerging threat to papaya production in Mauritius. This study was funded by the University of Mauritius and the European Union under the project “Enhancing climate resilience in agriculture for improved food and nutrition security through research, innovation and training in the Republic of Mauritius” under the contract number (FOOD 2019/406 182).
