AB  - Amoebic gill disease (AGD) is a potentially fatal disease of farmed and wild marine fish. The causative agent, Neoparamoeba perurans, is a ubiquitous free-living amoeba found from polar to subtropical marine environments. Neoparamoeba spp. are characterized by the presence of one or more eukaryotic symbionts. N. perurans colonizes fish gills, and the signs of subsequent disease include epithelial hyperplasia of the gills with epithelial desquamation and oedema, an increase in mucous cells and decreased chloride cell numbers. Major risk factors include high salinity (lower threshold: 20–25 ppt) and above-average temperatures (lower threshold: 4–8°C). PCR is required for definitive diagnosis, though gross gill scores in farms are a standard measure of disease severity. Affected fish show a reduction in maximum rate of O2 uptake. Freshwater bathing remains the main commercial treatment. Since first reports of the disease >40 years ago, AGD has become prevalent worldwide in salmonid aquaculture.
