AB  - <title>Abstract</title>
                <p>
                  Aquaculture production of Atlantic salmon is a rapidly growing food sector, offering numerous advantages over terrestrial livestock production. Yet, despite these advantages, the production efficiency, in addition to the health and welfare of marine raised Atlantic salmon is highly impacted by infectious diseases. In order to develop better preventative and therapeutic tools for infectious diseases, a better understanding of the immune response is required. In model species, the E3 ubiquitin ligase TRIM25 is a well characterised component of the host antiviral response, acting as both a sensor and a signal transducer for the RIG-like receptor pathway. In Atlantic salmon (
                  <italic>Salmo salar</italic>
                  ),
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  presents as two copies -ENSSSAG00000054152 and ENSSSAG00000046838 located on chromosome 2 (Ssa02) and 12 (Ssa12) respectively, and has been identified in multiple viral and viral mimic challenges as a virus-induced gene. To date, its role in the host response against viral pathogens remains uncharacterised. Viruses such as Infectious Salmon Anaemia Virus (ISAV) constantly threaten marine-raised Atlantic salmon, and novel strategies for prevention and treatment of viral outbreaks are fundamental for more efficient production. This research aims to characterise the function of the Atlantic salmon
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  to better understand the host response to viruses, leading to improvements in the management of viral diseases in salmon farming. Using Atlantic salmon head kidney 1 (SHK-1) cells and ISAV as a model infection, we show paralog-specific virus-inducibility and antiviral function of
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  . Whilst structural analyses showed that the two copies of
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  retain their functional domains, using CRISPR-Cas genome editing in combination with qPCR and epigenetic (ATAC and ChIP) sequencing we have demonstrated that Ssa02
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  exclusively has an impact on viral replication. Additionally, we demonstrate the conservation of the role of
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  in the RIG-like receptor pathway using RNA sequencing. Finally, we show that overexpression of
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  leads to protection from ISAV infection in a salmonid cell line. This research highlights the paralog specific antiviral role of
                  <italic>trim25</italic>
                  in fish, and paves the way for biotechnological applications to reduce the impact of viral outbreaks in salmon aquaculture.
                </p>
