AB  - Chromosomal structural rearrangements in several species have been shown to influence adaptation and plasticity in response to new environments. The extent to which this variation could be used in breeding programs to improve adaptability and productivity remains unexplored. Here, we investigated the existence of chromosomal structural variation in Tasmanian Atlantic salmon and its association with adaptation and production traits using a comprehensive genotypic and phenotypic dataset from a well-structured selective breeding program. High-density genotypes evidenced that translocation and fusion polymorphisms observed in North American wild populations are also segregating in the farmed Tasmanian population. Interestingly, a unique karyotype clustering pattern was observed for the fusion of chromosomes Ssa08 and Ssa29. We also observed evidence of fusion polymorphism of chromosomes Ssa26 and Ssa28, not commonly detected in wild North American populations. Chromosomal structural variation was significantly associated with resistance to amoebic gill disease, sexual maturation, survival and summer performance. For some traits, the average genetic merit difference between the opposite homokaryotypes exceeded 10% of the phenotypic mean. For each chromosomal rearrangement, no single karyotype group was superior across traits, and trade-offs between survival and production traits were observed. The inheritance pattern of the rearrangements indicated selection and mating decisions could be planned to favor the prevalence of specific karyotype(s) or the maintenance of karyotype diversity, depending on the breeding goals. Complementary studies using long-read sequencing methods are recommended for the validation and deeper understanding of our findings.
