AB  - Aquaculture is a rapidly expanding activity for animal protein production worldwide, serving as an alternative to the high demand for healthy food.However, like any intensive production system, aquaculture can create environments that stress animals, increasing the risk of the emergence and spread of infectious diseases.In contrast to the expansion of techniques for these cultivations, there is a scarcity of regulated medications available for use in aquaculture species, both for prophylaxis and treatment.Consequently, studies aimed at evaluating the potential regulation of new medications for this activity are necessary.Oxytetracycline (OTC), a bacteriostatic antibiotic, is currently indicated for the treatment of infections in crustaceans, lobsters, and salmonids as a regulated alternative for treating ulcer disease and furunculosis, as well as for catfish in cases of hemorrhagic septicemia.In this context, the present study aimed to develop procedures for incorporating OTC into fish feed, resulting in adequate homogeneity of OTC concentration among the feed pellets (CV of 0.71% to 4.92%), with incorporation efficiencies of the drug ranging from 70% to 86% and leaching rates of the drug into water varying from 42% to 9%.Such evaluations were made possible only after the establishment of an appropriate method, achieving an extraction efficiency of 95%, in conjunction with LC-MS/MS instrumentation.The analytical method was validated according to the recommendations of the MAPA (Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock and Supply) guidelines, demonstrating selectivity for OTC, linearity (R > 0.99), good repeatability and intermediate precision (CV < 2.76), and adequate accuracy (recovery) between 99.41% and 102.23%.The analytical method exhibited satisfactory robustness, with recoveries ranging from 98.6% to 102.2%.
