AB  - <jats:p>Jellyfish are among the multiple risk factors causing damage to gill epithelium in salmonids farmed in open-cage marine aquaculture systems. The present study used a salmonid 44K oligonucleotide microarray platform to profile changes in the transcriptome of rainbow trout epithelial cells (RTgill-W1) harvested 24 h after exposure to a filter-sterilized Aurelia aurita homogenate. The microarray analysis identified 675 up-regulated and 977 down-regulated probes at a False Discovery Rate of 1%. Based on the functional enrichment analysis of the microarray-identified genes, exposure to the jellyfish homogenate prompted an inflammatory and stress defense response in RTgill-W1 cells and promoted cell cycle arrest, apoptosis, and cell migration. Eighteen genes with putative roles in these biological processes were selected for RT-qPCR validation of the microarray results and analysis of their regulation after 0, 3, 6, 24, and 72 h exposure. This study is the first to explore the transcriptomic response of fish gill epithelial cells to A. aurita venom, a crucial step toward developing strategies to reduce the negative impact of jellyfish on finfish aquaculture.</jats:p>
