AB  - <jats:p>Reliable, non-intrusive monitoring of fish in commercial aquaculture cages remains challenging due to high fish density, turbidity, variable illumination. Moreover, in operational facilities, monitoring systems must operate continuously under limited computing resources and unstable environmental conditions.This work presents a systematic evaluation of deep learning architectures for integrated salmon instance segmentation and multi-object tracking under real production conditions. Two-stage (Mask R-CNN) and one-stage (YOLOv8, YOLOv9, YOLOv11) segmentation models are combined with state-of-the-art tracking-by-detection algorithms and evaluated using both the publicly available DeepFish dataset and proprietary datasets captured in operational salmon cages. Although Mask R-CNN achieves higher per-frame segmentation accuracy, YOLO-based models produce more temporally consistent detections, resulting in significantly improved tracking performance. Deployment experiments on an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin demonstrate sustained real-time throughput above 20 frames per second, with reduced-precision inference maintaining functional performance.Overall, the results show that temporal consistency, rather than peak pixel-level segmentation accuracy, is the key factor for reliable aquaculture monitoring. YOLOv8 and YOLOv11 combined with ByteTrack provide a balanced solution in terms of segmentation quality, tracking robustness, and computational efficiency, making them suitable for deploying scalable commercial fish farming systems.</jats:p>
