AB  - Environmental DNA (eDNA) metabarcoding provides a sensitive and non-invasive approach for monitoring fish diversity in urban rivers, but the accuracy of species-level annotation is strongly constrained by the coverage and taxonomic quality of reference databases. To improve the reliability of eDNA-based freshwater fish monitoring in China, this study constructed a China Freshwater Fish Mitochondrial Reference Database (CNFFmtDB) based on the FishBase checklist of Chinese freshwater fishes, with complete mitochondrial genome sequences integrated from NCBI GenBank and MitoFish. Using the North Canal as a case study, fish eDNA metabarcoding targeting the 12S rRNA V5 region was conducted, and taxonomic assignments based on CNFFmtDB were compared with those based on a broad NCBI metazoan 12S rRNA database (NCBI_12SDB). A dual-database fusion strategy was then developed to improve the reliability of fish species identification.The CNFFmtDB contained 1,298 non-redundant complete mitochondrial genome sequences representing 829 Chinese freshwater fish species. According to the FishBase taxonomic hierarchy, these species covered 33 orders, 78 families, and 301 genera, accounting for 46.4% of the 1,786 species in the FishBase-derived Chinese freshwater fish checklist. The comparison between CNFFmtDB and NCBI_12SDB showed that the two databases played complementary roles in eDNA annotation. NCBI_12SDB was useful for identifying non-target amplification signals, including humans, livestock, birds, and other non-fish taxa, whereas CNFFmtDB improved the local annotation of Chinese freshwater fishes and corrected misassignments caused by insufficient geographic coverage or confusion among closely related taxa in public databases.Using the dual-database fusion strategy, 31 valid fish species were detected in the North Canal, and the resulting inventory showed high consistency with historical conventional survey records. The fish community was dominated by tolerant and widely distributed cypriniform taxa, reflecting the typical community structure of highly urbanized river systems. Several non-native fish eDNA signals were also detected, including <i>Gambusia affinis</i>, <i>Clarias gariepinus</i>, <i>Ictalurus punctatus</i>, and <i>Oncorhynchus mykiss</i>. These detections indicate potential external inputs, aquaculture escape, release events, or local retention, but do not alone confirm the establishment of self-sustaining populations.Seasonal community analysis showed that more fish species were detected in the wet season than in the dry season. PERMANOVA indicated significant differences in fish community structure between two seasons. Environmental fitting further showed that dissolved oxygen, pH, and chemical oxygen demand were significantly associated with community ordination patterns, suggesting that oxygen conditions, alkalinity, and organic pollution gradients were important environmental correlates of fish community variation in the North Canal. Overall, this study demonstrates that the integration of CNFFmtDB with a broad public database can improve the local reliability of freshwater fish eDNA annotation in China. The proposed dual-database strategy provides methodological support for fish diversity assessment and non-native species risk identification in urban river ecosystems.
