AB  - <title>Abstract</title>
        <p>Bacterial outbreaks commonly challenge fish health in recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS). Many practitioners believe that recycled biofilters may be a potential source of pathogen contamination during subsequent cycles of fish growth. Biofilters undergo rigorous chemical disinfection protocols, known as “knockout” procedures, as a precaution to neutralize the carry-over of infectious agents. However, the impact of these disinfection protocols on the re-establishment of nitrifying bacterial biofilms within plastic biofilter carriers remains unknown. In this study, a moving bed biofilter from a recirculating aquaculture system (RAS) was used over time during a complete production cycle of Atlantic salmon (<italic>Salmo salar</italic>) smolts, before their transfer to seawater. The analysis employed next-generation sequencing of 16S ribosomal RNA gene amplicons, alpha diversity metrics, and their correlation with fish health and water quality. Sampling was conducted every 15 d to collect data from biofilter carriers and the biofilter water. Our findings revealed that the “knockout” protocols affected the composition and abundance of nitrifying bacterial communities within the biofilter carriers. Contrary to the logic supporting aggressive “knockout” procedures, significant out-growth of freshwater bacterial pathogens, e.g., <italic>Aeromonas salmonicida</italic>, was not observed in the biofilter carriers when the RAS was cleaned using low salt conditions. Our findings corroborate those of previous reports showing that low salinity (2–5‰) may influence bacterial diversity and abundance during biofilm regrowth. Our findings suggest that aggressive disinfection may be unnecessary for safe and functional biofilm re-establishment in RAS.</p>
