AB  - Monitoring fish growth in Recirculating Aquaculture Systems (RAS) is essential for optimizing production and ensuring animal welfare. However, traditional manual measurement methods are labor-intensive and induce significant stress in fish. This thesis presents a non-invasive, vision-based pipeline for automated juvenile Atlantic salmon length estimation using stereo camera imagery captured under realistic aquaculture conditions. The use of stereo imaging enables depth-aware observations, improving measurement consistency within dynamic tank environments. The proposed approach integrates detection, segmentation, and geometric analysis into a unified framework. Fish are localized using a YOLO-based detector, followed by precise contouring via the Segment Anything Model (SAM). To account for body curvature and partial occlusions, skeletonization is applied to the segmentation masks to extract the medial axis for accurate length estimation. This work provides a non-invasive solution for continuous growth monitoring in RAS—reducing technician workload, enhancing animal welfare, and establishing a foundation for real-time, automated aquaculture management.
