AB  - <jats:p>The aquaculture industry is exploring sustainable alternatives to fishmeal, with insect meals (IMs) emerging as promising options due to their high-quality proteins, lipids, and immunostimulatory compounds, such as chitin. This study evaluated how full-fat insect meals (IMs) affect the histomorphometry of Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) mucosal tissues (intestine, skin, and gills), their barrier function, and immune gene expression to explore their immunomodulatory potential. Two insect species were tested: black soldier fly, Hermetia illucens (HI), and yellow mealworm, Tenebrio molitor (TM), formulating into five diets: a control diet (CTL) with fish meal 20%, fish oil 13%, rapeseed oil 6%, soy protein 20%, and wheat gluten 14%; and four insect-based diets- LHI (5% low HI), HHI (10% high HI), LTM (15% low TM), and HTM (30% high TM)- where the mentioned other ingredients were replaced. The quadruplication of each dietary group continued for 74 days. Post-trial, samples from the distal mid-intestine (DI), skin, and gills were analysed for structure, barrier function, and immune gene expression related to mucin and antimicrobial peptides (AMPs). The HHI group showed notable changes in tissue structure, as indicated by principal component analysis, suggesting a beneficial effect. Mucosal assessment revealed increased mucous cell area, volumetric density, and barrier integrity in the HHI group. Immune cell counts in the intestine and skin were higher in the HHI group, along with increased cell proliferation in the distal mid-intestine. Mucin, AMP, and other key immune-associated gene expression were elevated across all dietary groups compared to CTL, particularly in the HHI group, which also showed significant upregulation of muc2 and cath1 in the distal intestine; muc5b in the skin; and muc2 in the gills. Overall, the results indicate that the HHI diet effectively modulated structural health, mucosal barrier function, and immune responses in Atlantic salmon.</jats:p>
