AB  - Genus Mycobacterium enters a dormant state in adverse environments, such as under anaerobic conditions and temperature extremes. Nontuberculous mycobacterial infections in cultured fish have caused damage in the aquaculture industry for decades. Disease outbreaks usually occur in specific seasons, but mycobacteria have also been detected in healthy fish outside the outbreak season. Therefore, the development of clinical signs and the mortality of farmed fish with mycobacteriosis may be affected by changes in water temperature in the aquaculture environment. In the present study, we aimed to elucidate the influence of temperature on the status of Mycobacterium salmoniphilum cultured in vitro as a model bacteria. Changes in its pathogenicity in the rainbow trout Oncorhynchus mykiss host, when reared in different water temperatures were also examined. We found that M. salmoniphilum entered a dormant state under low temperatures, and rainbow trout reared in low water temperatures did not show clinical signs of infection, despite being given a lethal dose of M. salmoniphilum . The gene expression levels of Th1-related cytokines were not upregulated in rainbow trout reared in lower temperatures and challenged with M. salmoniphilum . This evidence suggests that rainbow trout do not induce a immune response against dormant M. salmoniphilum ; thus, the bacteria probably cause latent infections in low water temperatures. Unlike homeothermic animals, poikilothermic animals body temperature is an important factor controlling the physiological activity of pathogens that reside in the host. Therefore, it is important to consider the influences of environmental factors, such as temperature change, on host pathogen interactions when developing countermeasures against mycobacteriosis.
