AB  - <jats:p>Image-based counting is increasingly used for automated monitoring of parasitic salmon lice (Lepeophtheirussalmonis) to coordinate pest control and assure regulatory compliance in salmon aquaculture. Machine-learning models for detection and classification of lice are trained based on human annotations and thus ultimately depend on human judgement. Yet the extent to which observers agree in their assessment of image material, particularly under changing environmental conditions, has not been investigated in scientific literature. To address this gap, image sequences were collected from a wide range of commercial farming conditions and assigned to distinct quality categories. Inter-observer variation was then examined to assess the influence of sequence quality on agreement between trained analysts performing lice counts. Overall, sequence quality assessment showed moderate inter-observer agreement based on Cohen’s kappa indicating some variability in categorical assessment. During lice counting, analysts showed high reliability, as reflected by intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC), with exact agreement increasing as sequence quality improved. Results further suggest that consistency remains high even though analyst uncertainty increased for sequences of reduced quality. This is relevant for regulatory frameworks, as image-based lice monitoring may be used to support compliance reporting and management decisions, and small differences in observer judgement can become important when counts are close to operational or regulatory thresholds. The implications of these findings for development, improvement and on-site application of automated detection systems are further discussed in the context of updated regulatory frameworks that allow for technical solutions.</jats:p>
