AB  - In order to solve the problem of excessive fat deposition in the visceral mass and high deformity rate of Oncorhynchusmykiss cultured in closed-circulation aquaculture, a study on water quality control of recirculating aquaculture systembased on intelligent optimization was proposed. This paper was to explore the effects of dietary fat nutritiononthegrowth, fat metabolism and related enzyme gene expression of Oncorhynchus mykiss cultured in closed-circulationaquaculture. A single-factor design was adopted, i.e., 4 fat levels - 12%, 15%, 18%, 21% (represented by groups Ⅰ, Ⅱ, Ⅲ, and Ⅳ), a total of 4 treatment groups, 3 replicates per treatment, and 25 fish per replicate. The experimental fishwerejuvenile Oncorhynchus mykiss with an initial body weight of (333.25±20.71) g, and the experimental period was 77days. The experimental results showed that compared with the last three groups, the weight gain rate of the experimental fishin Group I was significantly increased by 17.20%~33.17%, and the feed coefficient of Groups I and II was significantlylower than that of Groups III and IV by 6.20%~20.56% (P&lt;0.05); the activity of lipase FAS decreased with the increaseof dietary fat level, while the activity change characteristics of lipase LPL, HL and L-CPTⅠ were opposite (P&lt;0.05). Among them, the FAS activity of group Ⅰ was significantly higher than that of group Ⅲ and group Ⅳby 7.5%and8.7%respectively; the L-CPTⅠ activity was significantly lower than that of the latter three groups by 13.08%~16.35%; theLPLactivity of group Ⅳ was significantly or extremely significantly higher than that of the former three groups by18.8%~44.7% (P&lt;0.05 or P&lt;0.01); the mRNA expression of FAS in the liver of group I was significantly higher thanthat of the latter three groups by 48.29%~55.15% (P&lt;0.05); LPL showed the opposite characteristics, group Ⅳwassignificantly higher than that of the former three groups by 36.72%~113.59% (P&lt;0.05 or P&lt;0.01); the FASmRNAexpression in the muscle of group Ⅰ was significantly higher than that of group Ⅳby 66.82%(P&lt;0.05); the LPLexpression abundance decreased first and then increased significantly with the increase of fat level (P&lt;0.05). Theseresults were synchronized with the changes of the activities of the two enzymes in the liver. Conclusion: With the changeof dietary fat level, the expression of FAS mRNA in muscle was more obvious and regular than that in liver, whiletheexpression of LPL mRNA in liver was more obvious than that in muscle. This experiment preliminarily determinedthat in the closed-circulation aquaculture mode, when the protein level of the feed of 330g rainbow trout was 45%, thefat level should not exceed 12%.
