AB  - <jats:p>Peach anthracnose, caused by a Colletotrichum spp. complex, is becoming a more problematic disease of peach in Georgia, U.S.A., possibly due to fungal resistance to the commonly applied quinone-outside-inhibitor (QoI) fungicides. Symptoms include sunken, brown to salmon-colored spots, often with prolific sporulation. Symptoms are observed just prior to or after harvest, though there is evidence to suggest that latent infections occur throughout the season. However, strong support for early season latent infections is not readily found in the literature. An on-farm trial was conducted in 2024 to cursorily review the timing of pathogen infection, as well as to determine the efficacy of captan for reducing levels of disease incidence. The study provides strong evidence of early season infection, while also determining that multiple, well-timed captan applications can provide excellent control.</jats:p>
