AB  - <jats:p>The rapid expansion of Chilean salmon aquaculture has been accompanied by high levels of antimicrobial use (AMU), primarily for the control of Piscirickettsia salmonis, the etiological agent of Salmonid Rickettsial Septicaemia (SRS). Increasing reports of reduced susceptibility to florfenicol raise concerns regarding antimicrobial resistance (AMR) and the sustainability of current disease management strategies. Although national surveillance programs exist, they operate largely at regional scales and do not incorporate spatially explicit risk differentiation within production areas. This study proposes a geospatial framework for risk-based surveillance of antimicrobial use (AMU) in Chilean marine salmon farming. Using national-level production, mortality, and antimicrobial consumption data from all marine grow-out farms in Los Lagos and Aysén regions between 2015 and 2024, AMU per productive cycle was quantified and integrated with spatial cluster analysis (Local Indicators of Spatial Association, LISA) to identify high-risk areas. Persistent spatial clusters (“hotspots”) of high AMU were identified, supporting the implementation of targeted surveillance and risk-based management strategies. This framework provides the first nationwide spatially explicit approach to antimicrobial risk monitoring in aquaculture production systems and offers a scalable model for AMR mitigation under a One Health perspective.</jats:p>
