AB  - <jats:p>The study evaluated cross-isolate protection and efficacy of different vaccination strategies utilizing two commercial vaccines with ISA components in Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) against North American (NA) and European (EU) genotypes of ISAv. Two commercial ISA vaccines containing distinct genotype components were evaluated, with challenge isolates representing the two genotypes recovered from confirmed outbreaks in Eastern Canada. AlphaJect Micro®7 ISA contained an EU genotype, while a North American commercial vaccine (NACV) contained an NA genotype. Four strategies were tested: (1) a single intraperitoneal dose of AlphaJect Micro® 7 ISA; (2) AlphaJect Micro® 7 ISA followed by NACV booster administered approximately 500 degree-days later; concurrent administration of both vaccines; and a sham control. At 1000 degree-days, vaccinated fish were cohabitation-challenged using Trojan/shedder fish injected with NA- or EU-ISAv, and mixed with naïve cohabitants in replicated common-garden tanks. Fish were observed for morbidity and mortality for 61 days post-challenge. All vaccinated groups exhibited significantly higher survival compared to the sham control against both EU- and NA-ISAv, demonstrating cross-isolate protection across vaccine strategies.. The booster group produced the highest survival against NA-ISAv, but the survival among the vaccinated groups was not significant. Cross-protection differences between strategies were modest for NA-ISAv but pronounced for the EU isolate. AlphaJect Micro 7 ISA alone or with NACV booster had significantly higher survival against EU-ISAv than fish injected with both vaccines simultaneously and sham controls. Finally, the vaccine strategies did not differentially affect growth or Speilberg scores, indicating the safety of all tested vaccination strategies.</jats:p>
