AB  - <jats:p>Future growth within the Scottish salmon aquaculture will be dependent on its environmental sustainability, which means that, among other considerations, aquaculture operators must ensure that sea lice originating from farms do not cause unsustainable mortality to wild salmon. However, impacts of sea lice induced mortality on wild salmon are difficult to measure directly. Management must be informed by a set of high-quality (calibrated) and well-documented sea lice dispersal models. Here, we have documented the sensitivity analyses undertaken to assess the variance and fit of a set of models used by industry, government and academics for sea lice dispersal modelling, each using different particle tracking models with the same hydrodynamical forcing in this case. Loch Linnhe, a fjordic system in the west coast of Scotland, has been used as a test area, due to available historical empirical data for model validation. The analyses carried out increased our understanding of how the model outputs which estimate sea lice density in space and time are related to and influenced by the various input parameters tested. Lice swimming behaviour, specifically small scale diel vertical migration was identified as an important variable in simulating the distribution of sea lice in the environment. The source type (used to calculate number of lice larval released from each site) influences how well the model correlates to the field data. Releasing lice larvae at a rate corresponding to the Scottish industry Code of Good Practice threshold for management intervention of 0.5 adult female lice per fish, resulted in 10.7% more lice larvae in the system than in simulations run with observational lice data. This may be useful to managers/regulators in understanding the likely hotspots for lice in the water column, should lice loads on all farms reach these intervention levels. However, inference on the actual lice loads using a ”variable” source based on lice counts provided a better fit to the field data. There is a requirement for comprehensive empirical data collection in all areas where aquaculture occurs to assess model suitability within each system.</jats:p>
