AB  - <jats:p>The oxygen consumption of Atlantic salmon depends on digestion activity in addition to factors such as fish size, swimming speed and temperature. Feeding rate in pen based salmon farms can therefore affect the dissolved oxygen (DO) levels in cages, and in turn the risk of the fish experiencing hypoxic conditions. Earlier efforts in modelling the spatiotemporal variation in farm DO levels have captured many of the relevant factors, but neglected the effect of digestion activity. In this model study we aimed to use a mathematical model to 1) quantify the effect of digestion activity-driven variability in DO consumption on the DO levels in net pens, and 2) quantify the effect of fasting on DO levels in net pens. Results show that the daily variation in DO consumption caused by variability in digestive activity can significantly affect the risk of hypoxic conditions in parts of the daily cycle. Furthermore, the model indicates that reduced DO consumption under fasting has a clear impact in DO levels, and may thus significantly reduce the risk of the fish experiencing hypoxic conditions.</jats:p>
