AB  - For decades, public and, at times, scientific debate has placed fisheries and aquaculture on opposing sides. On one hand, capture fisheries are viewed as a declining activity, pressured by overfishing and the vulnerabilities of natural stocks due to climate change. On the other hand, aquaculture is frequently promoted as a solution to global food insecurity, yet is often criticized for its environmental impacts-such as water eutrophication, mangrove destruction, and, ironically, its dependence on wild-caught fishmeal to feed carnivorous farmed species.It is within this context that the present Research Topic, published in the Marine Fisheries, Aquaculture and Living Resources section of the journal Frontiers in Marine Science, is situated. The Research Topic collection addresses the most recent advances in the scientific debate on sustainability in seafood production, encompassing both aquaculture and fisheries."The application of intelligent production technologies, including automation of capture operations, artificial intelligence feeding systems, IoT-based water quality monitoring, and disease prediction based on multi-source big data, can enhance fisheries production efficiency, optimize resource allocation, and reduce ecological risks. Chen Li et al. showed that the smart fishery not only improves the economy and fishermen's income, but also benefits environmental conditions in China.The use of modern bioinformatic technology, such as machine learning and neural networks, also contributes to fishery improvement. Yildiz et al. provided the first systematic analysis of Automatic Identification System gaps in Black Sea fisheries, offering valuable insights into the causes of these data gaps and the implications for fisheries management. By integrating spatial and temporal analyses with machine learning techniques, the findings contributed to monitoring strategies, regulatory enforcement, and sustainable resource use.In this context, Hu et al. developed an intelligent forecasting model for aquatic production based on an artificial neural network. This study also highlights the key drivers affecting aquatic production, including economic, climatic, and demographic factors.Modern biotechnology also contributes to improving aquaculture and fishery production, especially in animal reproduction. Molecular analyses enable more precise control over reproductive management in farmed fish. Early sex identification of fish improves husbandry decisions and production efficiency in aquaculture. In this context, Hu et al. analysed the foxl2 gene from Chinese sturgeon (Acipenser sinensis) in relation to sex differentiation, thereby contributing to artificial fish reproduction and caviar production.The analysis of the population dynamics and characterization of the fishery generate important data for the sustainability of the seafood resource. The study by Trindade et al. characterized the size composition of the goliath catfish (Brachyplatystoma vaillantii) captured by the Amazon industrial fleet, as well as the vulnerability of juveniles in relation to the spatial and seasonal pattern of the Amazon estuary. This commercially important species from Northern Brazil is currently overfished. The study highlights the need for more effective spatial and temporal fisheries regulations. Similarly, with a focus on sustainability, Carneiro et al. characterize the strategies of small-scale artisanal fishers in the Eastern Amazon.In China, Lauden et al. have examined the population dynamics and seasonal variation (winter-summer) in the biological characteristics of black sea bream (Acanthopagrus schlegelii) to contribute to the stock management.The small-scale octopus fishery presents a specific assessment challenge; however, Prince et al. developed and tested a novel size-based data-poor assessment methodology, demonstrating its applicability in Indonesian fisheries for Octopus cyanea. This study contributes to the octopus stock data and management.One of the main concerns associated with trawl shrimp fisheries is the ecological impact, primarily due to the unintentional capture of non-target organisms. Peixoto et al. evaluated the impacts of industrial bottom trawl for the southern brown shrimp on the Amazon Continental Shelf in Northern Brazil. The findings enable the development of appropriate management strategies to reduce mortality and plan measures that support sustainable fishing practices.Global warming is threatening life on Earth and seafood production, and seaweeds play a central role in climate regulation and carbon sequestration. The study by Zhang et al. investigated the effects of light intensity on seaweed carbon assimilation and release in two strains of Pyropia haitanensis.Finally, salmon cage aquaculture has a substantial impact on the seabed. Wang et al. analyzed chemical and biological variables directly beneath and in surrounding areas, contributing data to support new regulations in Norway.In summary, the body of work assembled in this Research Topic advances a compelling and unified message: the sustainability of future seafood production hinges on overcoming the historical divide between fisheries and aquaculture research. Across diverse geographic and ecological contexts, the included studies demonstrate that intelligent production technologies, modern bioinformatics, molecular biotechnology, population dynamics modeling, and ecological impact assessments are not mutually exclusive domains but rather complementary tools within an integrated scientific framework.Key findings indicate that artificial intelligence and neural network models can optimize aquatic production forecasting; molecular approaches, such as sex determination gene analysis, enhance reproductive management in aquaculture; and refined population assessments provide critical data for the management of overfished species, from the Amazon to the Black Sea. Concurrently, methodological innovations for data-poor fisheries and empirical evaluations of benthic impacts from both trawling and cage aquaculture underscore the importance of evidence-based regulatory measures.Collectively, these contributions reaffirm that addressing the overlapping pressures of climate change, resource competition, and spatial conflict demands a holistic approach. Only by integrating capture fisheries and aquaculture within a shared scientific and governance agenda can the international community navigate the historic tipping point now facing global seafood production.
