AB  - <jats:p>Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus) exhibits highly variable reproductive performance compared to other salmonids. Sperm motility is a critical determinant of fertilisation success, yet temporal changes in motility patterns during the breeding season remain poorly understood. We assessed sperm quality during commercial roe production by sampling 49 males over three consecutive weeks, tracking changes in ten distinct motility subpopulations throughout the activation period using Computer-assisted sperm analysis (CASA). We compared tank water and AquaBoost® activator effects on motility parameters, examined whether breeding values influence sperm swimming patterns, and investigated whether different ovarian fluids trigger varying sperm responses from the same males. Sperm concentration averaged 3.19 ± 1.91 billion sperm/mL with no significant weekly variation. Sperm concentration emerged as the dominant predictor of activation success (odds ratio = 3.10). AquaBoost® activator significantly extended motility duration by 6.5 seconds compared to tank water and promoted rapid-swimming sperm, while water favoured medium-velocity categories. Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel analysis revealed significant temporal shifts in motility subpopulations, with Medium Super Progressive sperm showing consistent increases across weeks (θ = 2.66 for AquaBoost®, θ = 2.44 for water). Progressive motility at 10-14 seconds declined across the sampling period for both activators. Breeding values showed no detectable effects. Ovarian fluid effects were week-specific and temporally restricted, with significant male×ovarian fluid interactions detected in five motility classes during the 25-second post-activation. Our findings demonstrate that temporal subpopulation analysis might reveal patterns masked by average motility parameters, providing new insights relevant for both natural spawning and artificial fertilisation protocols.</jats:p>
