AB  - <jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>
                  <jats:p>More sustainable food production and consumption is required to combat climate change and to ensure future food security. Here we examine how switching/adapting protein sources can have a positive effect on both human and environmental health by exploring the impact on climate from changing our diet to increase our seafood consumption in line with NHS dietary guidance in the United Kingdom (UK) (the Eatwell Guide) and the EAT-Lancet Planetary Health Diet. Using our calculations we demonstrate that the UK has a high consumption of meat and meat products resulting in the current level of consumption which is 2–3 times higher than recommended guidelines of the Eatwell Guide and Planetary Health Diet. Over a twenty-year period, UK diet data from food purchases have shown a reduction in diet related carbon emissions following a maximum emission in 2003. This also corresponds to a decrease in meat consumption, though in total, consumption of animal sourced proteins is still responsible for 76% of emissions. Modelling for alterations and reductions of protein in the UK diet has a significant impact on carbon emissions. We modelled future predictions from 2021 to 2050 and our results demonstrate that comparing with the time point in 2021 a single substitution of beef with salmon reduces carbon emissions by 28% per person per week and reducing protein from meat and dairy led to a reduction of 39% per person per week compared with 42% (Eatwell Guide) and 49% (Planetary Health Diet) per person per week. These changes, if implemented, would be highly advantageous in contributing to national targets of reduced carbon emissions. We include a beef to salmon swap which represents a simple and consumer-possible change and discuss the changes and consequences of aquaculture in providing the additional quantities required for such a dietary swap.</jats:p>
