AB  - <jats:p>Antimicrobial use in salmonid aquaculture must be optimized to maintain therapeutic efficacy while limiting environmental impacts. This study evaluated zinc-alginate microparticles (MP) as an oral delivery system for oxytetracycline (OTC) in rainbow trout, focusing on formulation optimization, release kinetics and bioavailability. Microparticles were produced in a single step by combined ionic gelation and atomization, using a fixed OTC load (1.19 g) and varying alginate concentrations (0.5, 1.0 and 2.0 g). The resulting MPs showed elongated, invaginated morphologies (8.1–13.6 µm), highly negative zeta potentials (−56 to −58 mV), production yields ≥57% and association efficiencies &gt;95%. OTC loading was inversely related to alginate content. In vitro release testing with USP apparatus 4 demonstrated that increasing alginate concentration markedly slowed drug release; the 2 g formulation (MP2-OTC) released only about 20% of the encapsulated OTC over 72 h. Pharmacokinetic studies in rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) showed that MP2-OTC achieved a sustained-release profile compared with a commercial OTC formulation, with a reduced Cmax (200.46 vs. 337.37 µg/mL), doubled Tmax (24 vs. 12 h), lower absorption and elimination rate constants (Ka and Ke 0.015 h−1), and a 34% higher total drug exposure (AUC0–∞ 17 618 µg·h/mL). These results indicate that zinc-alginate MPs can extend systemic OTC residence time and improve oral bioavailability in rainbow trout, supporting this platform as a promising strategy to enhance antimicrobial therapy and reduce dosing frequency and environmental loading in salmonid aquaculture.</jats:p>
