AB  - <jats:p>Rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) is a cold-water species of major economic importance and widely farmed but elevated temperatures significantly reduce its summer survival and production performance. Gaining a deeper understanding of how rainbow trout regulate their biological responses will clarify the links between heat-stress signaling pathways and adaptive mechanisms. Here, we investigated the effects of two acute thermal stress (21ºC and 23ºC) on growth and blood proteomic in rainbow trout (~61 g) fed with different levels of krill meal (0, 25, 50, 75 and 100%). In Phase I, all fish were fed experimental diets for 70 days. Following this, two replicates maintained the same temperature, while remaining tanks underwent a temperature increase from 14°C to 21°C over 5 days. Fish were hand-fed to satiation twice daily for 7 days at 21°C (Phase II, first heat stress). Subsequently, a second heat stress raised temperature from 21°C to 23°C over two days, with fish hand-fed to satiation for 7 days at 23°C. Blood proteomic analysis showed that fish fed with KM7.5 and KM10 exhibited significant molecular adaptations supporting thermal resilience during Phase III. Enhanced oxygen transport was evident through strong upregulation of haemoglobin subunits (α1: log₂ FC 1.98 in KM10 vs 0.78 in KM7.5) and β subunits, facilitating aerobic metabolism under heat stress. Antioxidant defense systems, including glutathione peroxidase, peroxiredoxins, and thioredoxin pathways, were elevated in fish fed KM10 at 23°C, reducing oxidative damage. Metabolic flexibility was indicated by increased expression of carnitine-related proteins (KM10: 1.57; KM7.5: 1.51) and enzymes involved in fatty acid oxidation and the TCA cycle. Collectively, these findings highlight integrated physiological and molecular strategies enabling rainbow trout fed with krill meal to maintain homeostasis under challenging high temperature stress.</jats:p>
