AB  - <jats:p>Potato pink rot, caused by Phytophthora erythroseptica, is a soilborne disease that affects both the quality and quantity of potatoes. Infected tubers develop rot with a characteristic salmon-pink discoloration upon exposure to air. To assess the susceptibility of various potato clones to pink rot, a field trial was conducted in the 2024 growing season at the Aroostook Research Farm in Presque Isle, ME. This study evaluates 32 potato clones and 13 commercial varieties under inoculation of the pathogen, providing valuable insights for breeding programs and disease management strategies aimed at improving pink rot resistance in potato production.</jats:p>
