AB  - This paper presents the planning and execution of the breakthrough phase for Norway’s first land-based salmon farming facility constructed entirely in hard rock. Constructed by Leonhard Nilsen & Sons (LNS) for Andfjord Salmon, the project involved excavation of 4.4 km of tunnels, including over 700 meters directly under the ocean, with cross-sections up to 73 m 2 . The breakthrough was the most critical engineering challenge, requiring precise blasting and robust reinforcement to safely connect the tunnel system to the Atlantic Ocean under significant hydrostatic pressure and minimal rock cover. An expert group comprising the client, consultants, contractor and explosive supplier was established to ensure quality control and risk mitigation, with additional geological investigations and adjustments to the breakthrough point based on site condition. The final phase included extensive probe drilling, pre-grouting and custom-designed plugs to prevent uncontrolled water ingress. Safety was prioritized through measures such as evacuation protocols, safety zones and warning blasts to protect the animal life at sea. The breakthrough was executed with a high degree of control, achieving the desired tunnel geometry. Both, inlet and outlet tunnels, were designed with steep gradients and large cross sections to optimize hydraulic performance, and the final breakthrough areas was between 46 to 50 m 2 . The experience demonstrates that targeted planning, interdisciplinary collaboration and the application of the Norwegian tunnelling methods enable safe and precise breakthroughs even in complex geological settings. The lessons learned provide valuable guidance for future subsea tunnelling projects and highlight the potential for innovative use of underground space in land-based aquaculture. This case sets a new reference for safe and efficient tunnel breakthroughs beneath the sea, emphasizing the importance of expertise, risk management and collaboration.
