AB  - <jats:p>A 60-day feeding trial evaluated meals derived from discarded mushroom biomass, including stems and non-marketable mushrooms, generated during commercial cultivation and processing of Agaricus bisporus (AB) and Pleurotus ostreatus (PO) as lower-impact alternatives to soybean meal (SBM) in rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss, BWi: 22.1 ± 0.3 g) diets. Five isoproteic (47%) and isolipidic (23%) diets were tested: control diet and four diets replacing 25%-50% of SBM with AB or PO meals (AB25, AB50, PO25, PO50). Fish fed AB25 diet showed growth performance, feed intake (FI) and nutrient utilization comparable to control (p &gt; 0.05), whereas AB50 reduced growth (p &lt; 0.05) without affecting FI or nutrient utilization (p &gt; 0.05). In contrast, PO inclusion caused a progressive decline in these parameters, more pronounced at PO50, accompanied by increased hepatosomatic index and reduced perivisceral fat index (p &lt; 0.05). Apparent protein digestibility decreased in AB50 diet (p &lt; 0.05). In PO-fed fish, digestive enzyme activities reflected adaptive responses to reduced FI, including lower pepsin and higher total alkaline proteases, trypsin, lipase, aminopeptidase-N and maltase activities. Hematological and plasma markers remained within normal ranges, and liver histological organization was similar among treatments (p &gt; 0.05). Hepatic glutathione reductase activity increased in PO diets (p &lt; 0.05), whereas AB diets remained comparable to control group (p &gt; 0.05).  Overall, mushroom by-product meals were physiologically safe for O. mykiss, while AB meal emerged as a promising circular alternative to SBM, supporting up to 25% replacement without compromising growth performance, feed efficiency, or nutrient utilization.</jats:p>
