AB  - Piscirickettsiosis (SRS) is the infectious disease responsible for the highest mortality rates in Chilean marine salmon farms. Since 2012, the country has implemented a national fish health program for surveillance and control of SRS (PSVC-SRS). Identifying risk factors and quantifying their impact on SRS outbreaks is critical for improving disease characterization and strengthening risk-based surveillance and control strategies. We conducted a retrospective longitudinal study of 571 randomly selected production cycles of farmed Atlantic salmon ( Salmo salar L.) between January 2013 and June 2019, with outbreaks classified according to PSVC-SRS criteria. Time to SRS outbreak was estimated using Kaplan-Meier survival analysis and compared across attributes with the log-rank test. A parametric Weibull model was applied to identify independent predictors of outbreak onset. The overall median time to outbreak was 41 weeks (w) post-stocking (95% CI: 38–43 w). Fish stocking during spring was associated with delayed outbreak onset (median 47 w, 95% CI: 43–51 w), whereas initiation of antibiotic treatment when weekly SRS-related mortality ≥0.03% was associated with earlier outbreaks (median 26 w, 95% CI: 24–28 w). In the final parametric Weibull model, two independent variables remained significant: stocking season and timing of SRS treatment initiation. Specifically, farm cycle stocked in spring and initiating antibiotic treatment when SRS-related mortality <0.01% exhibit a median time to outbreak of 51.5 w post-stocking (95% CI: 48.3–54.8 w). This study contributes to a better understanding of SRS outbreak dynamics and offers an epidemiological assessment of the disease within the framework of an official national health program.
