AB  - According to the United Nations (UN) estimates, the world population will be 8.5 billion by 2030 (ESCAP, 2022). By the end of this decade, there will be more than 1.5 billion people living in India, and this number will rise until 2064. Population pressure leads to several threats like water paucity, desertification, high food crisis, and salinization. As a priority, the second goal of the 17 UN sustainable development goals (SDG) is SDG 2: “Zero hunger”: To end hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition, and promote sustainable agriculture. To meet the demand of the growing food crisis there is a need for sustainable culture systems. Aquaponic system offers the solution for the problem. Aquaponic system is an intensive, resource-efficient, closed-loop agricultural food production system, that symbiotically integrates aquaculture and hydroponics to produce fish and multiple cash crops with minimal area, reduced water, and fertilizer inputs. The wastes (faecal, uneaten feed) produced in the fish culturing system are converted by nitrifying bacteria into a form that plants can utilize for their growth, acting like a biofilter and remediating the culture water before recirculating into the fish tank. Aquaponics technology was developed in the 1970s focused on integrated fish production systems with vegetables. Now aquaponics is widely popular in many countries for commercial scale application mainly for many food fish like Oreochromis niloticus (Graber and Junge, 2009), catfishes (Endut et al., 2009), Oncorhynchus mykiss (Forchino et al., 2017), Cyprinus carpio (Shete et al., 2017), Lates calcarifer (Stathopoulou et al., 2021), and Piaractus mesopotamicus (Pinho et al., 2018). The culture of many species of ornamental fishes like Koi carps, Goldfish, gourami, and livebearers is also being practised in aquaponic system in many countries like Italy, etc. The demand for ornamental fishes is regularly increasing in India as the hobby of aquarium keeping is growing continuously (Jain et al., 2016), and the trend has been further bolstered by the inclusion of the ornamental fisheries sector as one of the focus areas under Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojna (PMMSY). However, the increasing urbanization in existing ornamental fish farming hubs of India like West Bengal, Tamil Nadu, and Kerla demands effective utilization of resources for high profitability. This has resulted in the integration of aquaponic technology for the culture of ornamental fishes at many farming facilities.
