AB  - A review of Applequist, W. L., editors. 2025. Love Them to Death: Turning Invasive Plants into Local Economic Opportunities. Missouri Botanical Garden Press, St. Louis, Missouri, USA. For field ecologists and land managers dealing with the onslaught of invasive species in natural areas, a new book edited by Wendy Applequist poses an intriguing concept: is it possible to incorporate practical everyday uses of invasive species as part of our management and control toolbox? In other words, can we turn invasive species into economic opportunities that may benefit society and concurrently reduce their numbers and ideally drive them to extinction? What if harvesting vines such as bittersweet or kudzu for basketry or collecting berries from common buckthorn for natural fabric dyes became more popular? Can invasive animals such as lionfish and mud crab be sourced as food for local restaurants? The hopeful optimist in me wants to agree, but the scientific record makes me pause, especially if an invasive species becomes so valued and integrated into society that its removal becomes even more difficult (more on this below). As an edited project, this book is structured into 13 chapters which include a mix of academic writing (such as legal regulations regarding “invasivory”, the human consumption of invasive species) and more practical, hands-on information (e.g., how to train cattle to consume invasive plant species). Consequently, the book features different writing styles, which once I got over my own initial hesitation, I ultimately found intriguing and even refreshing to read. The book begins with a brief introduction with the appropriate definition of an invasive species, and the requisite warnings about environmental impacts. The editor rightfully points out that the use of invasive species should only be considered a temporary endeavor, especially if it results in their successful eradication. Many of the chapters highlight the uses of a particular plant in its native range (such as kudzu in Chapter 1 or taro in Chapter 2, both excellently written) with the implication that it could be used in a similar way within the introduced range. Other chapters focus instead on one specific type of plant use with different species (such as Chinese medicine in Chapter 7, basketry in Chapter 11, or papermaking in Chapter 13). The level of detail in each of these chapters is exemplary. In particular, I appreciated the emphasis on responsible collecting of invasive plant material in natural areas to prevent any further spread (e.g., harvesting plants before they set seed, uproot entire plants when at all possible). I also felt myself unexpectedly pulled in by Katie Grove's chapter on basketry, as she explained her philosophy of the ethic of care in using invasive vines to make her baskets, with echoes of Robin Wall Kimmerer's Braiding Sweetgrass reminding us to appreciate nature. Chapter 7 on “Invasive Plants Used in Chinese Medicine” is noteworthy for its level of detail and organization; it contains not only the common, Latin, and Chinese names of each species used traditionally in Asia but also which part of the plant is used, its harvest techniques, and how to process the plant material for specific medical conditions. This information was nicely paired with the next chapter by Wendy Applequist, which provided clinical evidence of the medical efficacy of certain invasive or weedy plant species. I can see these two chapters alone serving as a resource for a Medical Botany undergraduate course. Such detail would have also been helpful in Chapter 4, which presented interesting culinary uses of invasive plant and animal species; however, only the common names were provided, so a reader may not be certain which species is recommended for human consumption. As someone who once ate a few honeysuckle berries and then quickly regretted it several days later, proper plant identification is of the utmost importance! Ultimately, the goal of the book is to persuade readers that a potentially effective approach to fighting invasive species is to “love them to death” by making them useful (albeit temporary) additions to our society, including as food, medicine, art, or even building material. While the editor is a proponent of using an invasive species to the point that it disappears from the landscape, that perspective does not seem to be shared by all contributors. In Chapter 9, the approach to common reed (Phragmites australis) is described as: “Rather than trying to eradicate invasive common reed [correctly described as European and African lineages], which is often futile or even devastating to the ecosystem, I suggest to…control its productivity through harvesting, as is done in many European areas.” (p. 188). Common reed is even suggested in this chapter to provide valuable ecosystem services in its North American range, but as someone who has seen wide swaths of Phragmites monocultures that outcompete most other plant species in wetlands, I simply cannot agree. This is a prime example of where regionality is crucial; European lineages can be of great value in their native range (as detailed nicely in this chapter) but that same genotype may spread unchecked once it arrives in a completely new environment in North America. Consequently, I began to wonder whether particular uses of a plant in its native range may not be as effective (or perhaps even more effective) within its introduced habitat. What if a plant's chemical composition and medicinal use reflected the climatic conditions, competitive ability, or even predator pressure of its native habitat, which might be completely different in a new location thousands of miles away? As I read this book, I found myself considering all the possibilities in which the spread of invasive species could potentially be curbed if more people used them, especially if an invasive species can be driven to extinction. This is certainly a tantalizing thought and goes beyond the typical tongue-in-cheek suggestion of “If you can't beat them, eat them!” However, as Sara Kuebbing and coauthors (Chapter 5) correctly pose, what happens if “we love it so much, we don't want to love it to death”? In some cases, invaders can become culturally integrated (such as wild boar or even wild horses) or, in other cases, an invader such as Atlantic salmon becomes economically profitable in some areas. They point out that a “win-win” situation of using invasive species is not always guaranteed. Consequently, I was persuaded that although more frequent use of some invasive species for specific purposes may help curb their spread, it must be examined on a case-by-case basis, as unintended consequences also have the potential to exacerbate the invasion problem. Ultimately, I found this book to be an intriguing read, with chapters that would each interest different audiences: researchers, land managers, farmers, herbalists, and even artists. Although I still have trouble considering invasive plant species as economic opportunities, I have no problem if they can be “loved to death.”
