AB  - <jats:p>There is an urgent need to reduce antimicrobial use in aquaculture. One method is through improved delivery systems. This study aimed to optimize the performance of oxytetracycline (OTC) loaded into zinc-alginate microparticles (MP), with a focus on prolonging release and increasing bioavailability in salmonids. Formulations were produced in a single step via combined ionic gelation and atomization; keeping OTC at a constant 1.19 g while varying alginate concentration (0.5, 1, or 2 g). The resulting microparticles exhibited elongated, invaginated morphologies ranging from 8.1 to 13.6 micrometers and had highly stable zeta potentials between -56 and -58 mV. OTC loading was inversely proportional to alginate content, achieving high yields (≥57%) and association efficiencies above 95%. In vitro release assays (USP apparatus 4) demonstrated that increasing alginate content significantly changed the release profile; with the 2 g formulation, MP2-OTC, releasing ~20% over 72 h. Pharmacokinetic analysis in rainbow trout (Oncorhynchus mykiss) revealed that MP2-OTC effectively transitioned to a sustained-release profile. Compared to commercial OTC, MP2-OTC significantly decreased Cmax (200.46 vs. 337.37 µg/mL) and doubled the Tmax (24 vs. 12 h). MP2-OTC showed lower absorption and elimination rates (Ka and Ke both 0.015h-1) and achieved a 34% higher total drug exposure (AUC0-∞ = 17618 µg*h/mL), indicating extended systemic residence time and improved bioavailability. These findings indicate that improved zinc-alginate MP represent a promising strategy to enhance the therapeutic efficacy of OTC while potentially reducing dosing frequency and environmental impact in aquaculture.</jats:p>
