AB  - Understanding the factors driving fish behavior, physiology, and survival is increasingly important during this period of unprecedented global change, given their implications for fisheries stability and ecosystem health. Habitat quality and quantity shape fish population dynamics and eco-evolutionary trajectories. Quantifying the habitat needs of fish across all life stages (and of their predators and prey) is challenging, however, and relies on diverse approaches such as field observations, laboratory experiments, genomics, chemical tracers, telemetry, and modeling. Successful integration of these data into management and policy requires open and constructive knowledge exchange between natural and social scientists, stakeholders, managers, and policymakers, and new tools to analyse and visualize these complex datasets. Building this social-ecological connectivity is particularly important in dynamic boundary systems (e.g., estuaries) and for protecting species characterized by trans-boundary movements (e.g., between rivers and seas, or across jurisdictional borders) if we are to maximize the benefits for nature and humans alike. Here, we present a collection of papers that deal with these topics in a Special Issue born from the Fisheries Society of the British Isles (FSBI) 2023 Symposium, “Fish Habitat Ecology in a Changing Climate” (see Figure 1), held at the University of Essex and co-convened by the Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science (Cefas). The overarching theme was inspired by a previous Jack Jones Lecture by Jonathan Rice, “Understanding fish habitat ecology to achieve conservation” (Rice, 2005), where he argues that “habitat science can provide the unifying concepts to bring together ecological studies of physiological tolerances, predator avoidance, foraging and feeding, reproduction and life histories.” Here, we broaden that definition to include the downstream effects of habitat (and habitat loss or restoration) on ecosystem services such as human food supply and nutrient requirements, and how humans and habitats interact in the space of fisheries and ecosystem management. The papers in this FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue fall under five major themes, which all have clear links to UN Sustainable Development Goals (e.g., Climate Action, Life Below Water), and the critical roles and services that fish provide (see Figure 2). Sustainable management of fish populations first requires an appreciation that they are not static entities and can move between different habitats that could provide key resources or shelter in different seasons or life stages. Quantifying fish movement and connectivity (Theme 1) is thus a key empirical and analytical challenge that needs to be overcome. Fish also do not exist in isolation, so managing a target stock requires understanding of how changes to ecosystems, particularly in their predators or prey, may underpin unexpected declines in the species of interest. Quantifying the role of fish in food webs (Theme 2) is therefore essential for a more holistic ecosystem-based management approach. Integrating data on single-species responses to changing habitat conditions with knowledge of movement, connectivity, and the wider food web will increase our capacity for understanding and predicting future fish distributions (Theme 3). Anticipating such changes will help to inform sounder management of fish and their habitats, not only for sustainable harvesting and development of contemporary habitats but also for safeguarding habitats that may be critical to the future health of populations and ecosystems. This integrated approach can also reveal the importance of protecting and restoring critical habitats that may support a key life stage or crucial resource and thus act as bottlenecks to population persistence in a changing climate. Therefore, integrating Themes 1–3 provides a comprehensive template for ecosystem-based management and restoration (Theme 4). Optimizing the management and conservation of commercially valuable fish stocks and their surrounding ecosystems will ultimately benefit not only our aquatic environments but also human health (through the provisioning and regulating services they provide) and socioeconomics (via the many jobs they support). Thus, quantifying, predicting, and managing the effects of climate change on fish habitat ecology is central to recognizing and preserving the objectives of “One Health” (Theme 5), that is, optimizing the health of people, animals, and our environment. Aquatic ecosystems are intrinsically connected by the flow of water that carries genes, organisms, and their waste products between them (Keeley et al., 2022). This functional connectivity is important for maintaining genetic and trophic connectivity among habitat patches and promoting resilience to disturbance; however, it is rarely incorporated into marine spatial planning (Cowen et al., 2007; Darnaude et al., 2024). This is partly because water bodies may span countries or even continents, but they are frequently managed at the national or regional level. Although river flows and ocean currents play a major role in maintaining connectivity between areas, fish can also swim countercurrent, creating multidirectional linkages that support food webs and ecosystem services at multiple spatiotemporal scales (Cavan et al., 2019; Flecker et al., 2010). Indeed, many fish species perform energetically costly migrations to maximize fitness, often seasonally between feeding and spawning areas and/or ontogenetically from nursery areas to the adult stock, as visualized by the classic migration triangle (Harden Jones, 1968). These movements often cross jurisdictional and ecological boundaries, creating challenges for monitoring and management (Hermoso et al., 2021; Speed et al., 2010). Furthermore, migratory fish have been heavily impacted by habitat fragmentation and degradation, climate change, and overexploitation, resulting in widespread declines, particularly among those that spend at least part of their life cycle in fresh water (Deinet et al., 2020). In the FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue, Franklin et al. (2024) review how climate change is expected to affect structural and functional connectivity in aquatic ecosystems, discussing the implications of predicted species distribution shifts in response to warming, hypoxia, and other stressors. They outline key challenges and solutions for managing aquatic connectivity in a changing climate that include the importance of nature-positive climate adaptation, strategic spatial planning, defining objectives under a shifting baseline, adaptive management, and performing monitoring at relevant spatiotemporal scales to detect changes through time. Indeed, although intraspecific biodiversity—such as the expression of multiple migratory contingents within a population (Secor et al., 2020)—underpins population resilience, key genotypic and phenotypic traits are often not monitored, resulting in their loss being cited as a “hidden biodiversity crisis” (Des Roches et al., 2021). In Willmes et al. (2024), the frequency of different migratory strategies within a Chinook salmon population in California was described over a 10-year period, for both juvenile and adult life stages. The results highlight the important connections between upstream and downstream habitats, as well as higher life-history diversity than expected despite more than a century of habitat loss and other anthropogenic stressors. The study also shows that climate-associated variation in migratory behavior and growth should be accounted for when setting a single baseline. Tracking fish movements in aquatic settings remains an ongoing challenge, but increasing numbers of studies are demonstrating the clear benefits of combining methods (otolith and genetic markers, biophysical modeling, and tagging) for assessing connectivity across life stages and ecological realms (e.g., Brophy et al., 2020; Michalsen et al., 2014; Willmes et al., 2024). The growth, movement, and phenology of any single fish species are inherently dependent on the structure and productivity of the wider food web. Thus, it is imperative to consider trophic interactions for a more complete understanding of how fish populations will respond to future climate change. For example, larger fish are widely predicted to be unfavored by warmer environments (Baudron et al., 2014; Eskuche-Keith et al., 2024; but see Audzijonyte et al., 2020) due to their higher metabolic demands and the inefficient transfer of energy through longer food chains (Barneche et al., 2021; Brown et al., 2004). However, the size and abundance of fish can actually increase with temperature if there is sufficient nutrient supply to support higher primary productivity and thus greater biomasses of their prey, helping them to overcome the energetic constraints of warmer habitats (Binzer et al., 2012; O'Gorman et al., 2017). Conversely, stronger feeding interactions with warming could lead to simpler food webs with fewer species and the loss of intermediate predators that may otherwise persist in the face of climate change (O'Gorman et al., 2023; Svensson et al., 2017). Thus, bottom-up or top-down control through the food web could play a key role in determining the persistence of fish populations in response to climate change, even if the individual species can tolerate or even thrive in the new conditions. In FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue, Thorpe et al. (2024) identify a current limitation in predictive fisheries research, whereby zooplankton are poorly represented in many models even though they are a key intermediary between primary production and fish communities. They show that models are more sensitive to the treatment of zooplankton than to a warming scenario of 2°C, illustrating the importance of accurately capturing food web dynamics for forecasting fish responses to climate change. The critical role of resource supply can even transcend ecosystem boundaries, with Massie et al. (2024) showing that freshwater subsidies to estuarine environments during low water levels help to increase the body condition of a prized game fish. Such food web knowledge can improve management decisions aimed at conserving ecologically and socioeconomically important species. The ability to move between and forage in habitats along a salinity gradient could also help some species to persist in the face of future increases in freshwater inundation to coastal estuaries (MacRae et al., 2024). Recognizing the importance of food web approaches is one thing, but incorporating them more widely into fisheries management and biomonitoring has been impeded by the difficulty and time-consuming nature of documenting trophic interactions and their plasticity in the face of environmental change. Rapid advances in DNA metabarcoding approaches could, however, provide the important bridge toward a more ecosystem-based approach to fisheries research (Canals et al., 2024). In the face of rapid environmental change, it is important to develop a quantitative understanding of the habitat needs of different fish species across all their life stages, and how different biotic and abiotic conditions shape their distribution and abundance. Using these relationships, we can better parameterize predictive models to forecast how fish and fisheries production will respond to alternative future global change scenarios. Warming, hypoxia, and habitat loss are among some of the many stressors faced by contemporary fish populations that have been linked to large-scale shifts in fish distributions to higher latitudes (Chen et al., 2011; Dahms & Killen, 2023), deeper depths (Currey et al., 2015), and higher elevations (Comte & Grenouillet, 2013). Collectively, these distributional shifts present challenges for transboundary governance and marine spatial planning (Palacios-Abrantes et al., 2022) and are predicted to have a disproportionate impact on protein and nutrient supply at lower latitudes (Lam et al., 2020). Accurately predicting how fish populations will respond to projected environmental change requires the collation and synthesis of data from a wide range of disciplines, including the laboratory- and field-based study of physiology and thermal tolerance limits, genetics and adaptive capacity, movement, and community and food web ecology (Waller et al., 2024). For example, using otolith-derived field metabolic rate estimates in Bluefin tuna, Trueman et al. (2023) showed that important nursery areas will become thermally limiting under future warming scenarios. For other fish species, habitat suitability modeling has demonstrated that a lack of suitable habitat means that shifting into deeper waters is not always a viable option (Rutterford et al., 2015). Vertical habitat compression is further exacerbated by the combined effects of warming, fishing pressure, and ocean deoxygenation, with elasmobranchs increasingly restricted to a narrower range of depths, as discussed in the extensive review by Waller et al. (2024) in the FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue. However, many studies in the Symposium Issue highlighted the diversity in fish responses to different stressors. For example, Mukherjee and Bhat (2024) empirically tested the influence of different temperature regimes on the physiology, foraging, and shoaling behavior of three tropical fish species and found that species displayed different responses to constant versus periodic exposure to elevated temperatures. Meanwhile, Ellis et al. (2024) showed that although the West of Scotland cod stock exhibited the expected poleward shift from 1985 to 2021, the trend was not shared by the Celtic or Irish Sea stocks. Ultimately, understanding individual- and population-level responses to changing environmental conditions and multiple interacting stressors will require innovative approaches that combine information from presence/absence and abundance data (Ellis et al., 2024; Shelton et al., 2022), tagging and sensor technologies (Waller et al., 2024), laboratory experiments (Killen et al., 2021; Mukherjee & Bhat, 2024), natural tags (Cordoleani et al., 2021; Trueman et al., 2023), and embracing machine learning and artificial intelligence to more accurately forecast future environmental conditions (Sonnewald et al., 2021). One of the major challenges to successful fish and habitat management involves building human social connectivity and strengthening communication between academia, stakeholders, managers, and policymakers to better support fish conservation and approaches such as natural capital and nature-based solutions. Monitoring, protecting, and restoring the critical habitats supporting fish stocks across their entire life cycle, particularly when they cross ecological or management boundaries, are also essential for more effective ecosystem management and spatial planning. About 30 years ago, the fishing industry and fisheries policy authorities focused mainly on harvesting fish as food. There is now an understanding of the need to ensure long-term food security within the context of global challenges faced by marine, coastal, and riverine environments due to climate change and biodiversity loss (Hák et al., 2016). For more than a decade now, fisheries management strategies have focused on targets contributing to healthy fish stocks and a productive marine environment to ensure the long-term future of sustainable commercial and recreational fishing sectors (Rice & Connolly, 2007). This includes the emergence of new tools to inform the decision-making process and management strategies (Graham et al., 2023), and sharing ownership of the management process with fishers as an incentive to fish sustainably using participatory fisheries management (Hart, 2021). As decision makers try to manage fisheries alongside other growing sectors occupying the marine space, such as offshore wind infrastructure, there is a need for evidence and scientific advice to help them manage activities and the ecosystem as a whole (Piet et al., 2021). In the FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue, Murray et al. (2024) present a perspective on the emerging issues in fisheries science and how collaborative platforms may offer solutions to address them in the context of rapidly changing climatic, ecological, and socioeconomic landscapes. Key themes addressed include the trade-off between the climatic benefits of offshore renewable energy and its impacts on fisheries, the value of genetics for quantifying stock structure and the role of gene flow in mitigating overexploitation, the transition from single-species to ecosystem-based management, and the ongoing issue of data availability as a limiting factor in fisheries management. Exploring new approaches to fill these data gaps is gathering pace. For example, Dawson et al. (2024) used otolith daily growth rings to demonstrate a 2-month mismatch between the fisheries closure period for European sea bass and the peak spawning window supplying juvenile bass to northern Wales. Multiple papers also highlight the importance of protecting and restoring the nursery grounds of commercially important species. Freeman et al. (2024) explore fine-scale habitat use in estuaries by newly settled sea bass, showing high abundances on sandflat areas, but higher stomach fullness indices in fish sampled on saltmarshes, whereas Dawson et al. (2024) show differences in body condition between two nearby estuaries in Wales. Collectively these studies suggest that habitat quality can vary over short spatial scales and that microhabitats within an estuary can fulfill different roles, for example, providing shelter from predation or improved foraging opportunities. This highlights the importance of protecting the full suite of habitat types within the coastal seascape to maximize opportunities for growth and survival at these critical early life stages. However, coastal restoration efforts should also consider the diversity of life histories and feeding strategies displayed by fishes that depend on these nearshore habitats, with ten Brink et al. (2024) using population modeling to show how different guilds (estuarine residents vs. marine estuarine-dependent species that only use nearshore areas as nursery grounds) can exhibit different somatic growth and survival responses to changes in food availability. Fish and other seafood are considered the fastest-growing traded products, reflecting changes in food and food the 2024). However, there is a growing body of evidence toward increasing on marine and aquatic environments fish For example, stressors such as marine emerging and affect fish behavior and health (e.g., physiology, growth, and on how this will impact global fish production and human health and Although there are strategic approaches to marine environments and 2020; 2019; integrated planning could help effects and ensure the long-term of fisheries and The of “One Health” together the health of animals, and the wider a to improve environmental and human health through the sustainable use of marine resources as a means to ensure global food security et al., et al., 2020). In the FSBI 2023 Symposium Issue, et al. (2024) in fish and providing new into the for and responses to increasing our understanding of the physiological for their habitat Meanwhile, (2024) the importance of health supply and food production systems in as a means to human and ecosystem health. For example, emerging fisheries management strategies could nutrient to humans in where fish is a critical of et al., 2022), whereas new population modeling approaches could develop fishing at the rate at which essential are 2024). These approaches to fisheries management will become increasingly for and sustainable food systems as we to achieve by are to have the 2023 FSBI Symposium, with the by University of Essex and of the for fish habitat ecology in a changing climate to have more interactio