AB  - Controlling salmon sea lice has been challenging due to the dynamic nature of the environment, fish welfare issues, and operational costs, highlighting the importance of proactive delousing strategies for the timely management of operations. This paper presents the development of data-driven machine learning (ML) models aimed at predicting delousing treatment events in Norwegian salmon aquaculture, formulating the task as a binary classification problem. Seven different ML models were developed and evaluated using an integrated dataset from 2020 to 2024. The dataset, collected from multiple sources, includes site production data, fish health records, and environmental conditions. A spatial-temporal analysis was conducted to examine treatment patterns across production areas. The ML models were implemented on a cloud-based platform and validated using a time-aware cross-validation approach. In addition, various sampling strategies were investigated to handle class imbalance during the training phase. The prediction results were evaluated using the average precision (AP) and AP lift metrics. SMOTE (SM) emerged as the best-performing resampling approach, leading to improved predictive performance across the ML models. The model’s performance hinged on the sampling strategies, spatial-temporal contexts, and dataset size. The pairwise comparison on the AP lift scores indicated that ensemble models showed comparable performance. Among the tested ML models, eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGB) model achieved the highest performance, with a mean AP lift score of 3.74.
