AB  - <jats:p>Recirculating aquaculture systems (RAS) with high water reuse accumulate fine solids (&lt;40–60 μm) and orthophosphate (PO4-P). Fine solids are typically removed using protein skimmers (PS), while PO₄-P is removed separately via end-of-pipe chemical coagulation. Integrating coagulation into the PS could allow simultaneous removal of both fine solids and PO₄-P in a single unit. The current study evaluated the use of calcium hydroxide (Ca(OH)₂) as a coagulant in PS in a freshwater RAS stocked with Atlantic salmon. A 7-week trial was conducted using six experimental RAS units with three treatments in duplicate: i) Control (Ctrl; tank, swirl separator, sump, biofilter, degasser), ii) PS (Ctrl + PS), and iii ) PSCa (Ctrl + PS + Ca(OH)₂). Each RAS was stocked with 40 fish averaging 43 g body weight. Growth performance (SGR and TGC), water quality (NH₄-N, NO₃-N, turbidity, PO₄-P, Ca, TOC, heterotrophic bacteria), biofilter efficiency (AOR), and PO₄-P removal were assessed. Survival was 100% in all treatments. PSCa had significantly lower PO₄-P in week 6 and week 7 than Ctrl, with 8 times greater PO₄-P removal during dosing than PS. TOC was reduced by 22% in PSCa and 14% in PS relative to Ctrl. Calcium in PSCa increased from 44 to 175 mg/L. This study shows that Ca(OH)₂ addition in PS can improve PO₄-P and TOC removal in freshwater RAS without adverse effects on fish performance, highlighting its potential as a multifunctional process integration strategy.</jats:p>
